    modelAnswer: "The diagram illustrates how plants create food from sunlight, air, water and minerals.\n\nOverall, this natural process begins with those four inputs entering the plant and ends with the release of oxygen and the conversion of sugar into starch for growth and repair. Water and minerals are taken up through the roots, while sunlight and carbon dioxide reach the leaves.\n\nSunlight is directed onto the leaves from above, and carbon dioxide is absorbed from the surrounding air. At the same time, rain falls and travels down into the soil, where water is taken in through the roots, together with minerals from the ground. These four materials therefore enter the plant by two routes: the leaves and the root system.\n\nOnce they have been absorbed, they are used inside the plant to produce two outputs. Oxygen is released from the leaves into the air, while sugar is formed. This sugar is then changed to starch, which is used for the plant’s growth and repair.",
