    modelAnswer: "The line graph shows employment patterns in the USA between 1930 and 2010, as a percentage of the labour force.\n\nOverall, farming, fishing and forestry declined throughout the period, while other services changed little and the remaining sectors rose. Farming also showed the sharpest change.\n\nIn 1930, farming, fishing and forestry accounted for about 75% of employment. The share declined only gradually until 1960, then dropped sharply to around 40% in 1980 and to only a few percent by 2010. Industrial employment stayed near zero until 1960, then climbed to about 30% in 1990 – overtaking farming – and finished at roughly 35%, the highest share. Technical work followed a similar rise from the same low starting point, ending at about 30%.\n\nSales and office work started at around 10%, dipped in 1940, and then rose to about 20% by 2000, where it remained. Other services peaked at roughly 15% in 1940 and then stayed close to 10% for the rest of the period.",
