    id: "w-task2-advertising-sameness",
    title: "Advertising Sameness",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "media",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Advertising discourages us from being different and individuals eventually look the same. To what extent do you agree or disagree?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Advertising is often accused of suppressing individuality by encouraging consumers to adopt the same appearance. Although commercial messages can produce visible trends, I largely disagree that they inevitably make people look alike, because modern advertising also promotes varied identities and consumers retain choice.\n\nAdmittedly, major campaigns can create temporary uniformity. Advertisers associate particular clothes, hairstyles, or devices with popularity and success, causing consumers to imitate the same constructed image. This pressure is especially strong among teenagers, who may fear social exclusion if they ignore a trend followed by their peers. Global fashion chains reinforce the effect by placing similar products in stores across numerous countries. As a result, the same trainers or clothing styles can become common in cities with distinct cultures. However, such resemblance is limited to certain products and periods; a fashionable item does not erase the many differences in how individuals combine, modify, and use it.\n\nMore importantly, contemporary advertising depends increasingly on market segmentation rather than a single ideal. Companies target consumers according to age, interests, culture, and lifestyle, presenting products as tools for expressing an identity. Digital platforms have intensified this process because small brands can reach specialised audiences without purchasing national television campaigns. Someone seeking handmade clothing, modest fashion, or environmentally responsible products can therefore discover alternatives that would have remained invisible. Furthermore, consumers interpret advertisements selectively. They may accept a recommendation for practical reasons while rejecting the values or appearance surrounding it. Advertising influences the options people notice, but it does not remove personal taste, cultural traditions, or the desire to stand apart.\n\nIn conclusion, advertising can encourage short-lived similarities when a product becomes fashionable, particularly among impressionable consumers. Nevertheless, its increasingly diverse targeting and the continued agency of buyers mean that it does not ultimately eliminate individual appearance.",
