    type: "opinion",
    topic: "society",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Some modern artists receive huge sums of money for the things they create, while others struggle to survive. Governments should take steps to resolve this unfair situation. To what extent do you agree or disagree?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "The art market rewards a small number of creators extremely well while others earn very little. Although this inequality is clear, I disagree that governments should attempt to equalise artists' incomes; their role should instead be to widen access to opportunities and protect basic labour rights.\n\nDirectly correcting income differences would require the state to decide how much artworks or artists deserve, a judgement that officials are poorly placed to make. Prices reflect a combination of reputation, scarcity, collectors' preferences, and commercial promotion rather than a measurable level of effort. Capping the price of a successful artist's work could therefore restrict voluntary transactions without improving the quality or diversity of art. Similarly, distributing grants simply to match private earnings would direct public money towards an occupation that people choose despite uncertain returns. Unequal outcomes alone do not justify government control of a legitimate market.\n\nNevertheless, public policy can make the route to an artistic career less dependent on personal wealth and industry connections. Governments can fund transparent, competitive grants for early-career artists, provide affordable studio and exhibition spaces, and support arts education in public schools. These measures give talented people time, facilities, and visibility without guaranteeing them a particular income. Authorities should also enforce contracts and copyright law so that galleries, platforms, and commercial clients cannot withhold payment or reproduce work without permission. A local exhibition programme that selects creators through independent panels, for example, can introduce unfamiliar artists to audiences who would otherwise encounter only heavily promoted names. Such intervention addresses unfair barriers while preserving artistic freedom and public choice.\n\nIn conclusion, governments should not try to eliminate the large income gap between artists because artistic value and market demand cannot be allocated fairly by officials. They should, however, ensure open access to funding, facilities, education, and legal protection.",
