    id: "w-task2-c-07-music",
    title: "Music",
    task: "task2",
    type: "two-part",
    topic: "culture",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>There are many different types of music in the world today. Why do we need music? Is the traditional music of a country more important than the international music that is heard everywhere nowadays?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Music appears in almost every society, despite enormous differences in style and purpose. It is needed both for emotional expression and social connection, and I believe traditional music has greater cultural importance than international popular music, although both remain valuable.\n\nAt an individual level, music gives form to feelings that may be difficult to express directly. People use energetic songs to motivate themselves, quiet pieces to reduce stress, and ceremonial music to process shared joy or grief. These personal effects also create collective bonds. A national anthem, a religious chant or even a concert can temporarily unite strangers through the same rhythm and emotion. Music is therefore not merely entertainment; it supports psychological well-being and marks significant stages of communal life. Its accessibility further explains its universal appeal, since a melody can communicate mood even when listeners do not share a language.\n\nBecause music helps communities express who they are, traditional forms deserve particular protection. They preserve local instruments, stories and performance techniques that cannot be replaced once the chain of transmission is broken. Vietnamese folk genres such as quan họ, for example, carry regional vocal patterns and social customs across generations. International music serves a different but still useful role: it exposes listeners to new sounds, gives young people common cultural references and encourages creative exchange. Nevertheless, globally marketed songs are already sustained by large commercial platforms, whereas traditional musicians often depend on schools, festivals and public institutions. Giving greater importance to local traditions is therefore necessary to maintain musical diversity, not to isolate a country from outside influence.\n\nIn conclusion, people need music to regulate emotion and strengthen social ties. International music enriches contemporary life, but traditional music is more important culturally because it safeguards a distinctive inheritance that global entertainment cannot reproduce.",
