    id: "w-task2-c-08-supporting-artists",
    title: "Supporting Artists",
    task: "task2",
    type: "discussion",
    topic: "arts",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Some people think that governments should give financial support to creative artists such as painters and musicians. Others believe that creative artists should be funded by alternative sources. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.</p>",
    modelAnswer: "There is disagreement over whether creative artists should receive public funding or rely on other sources of income. Although private finance can make the arts more responsive to audiences, I believe governments should provide targeted support for culturally valuable work that the market is unlikely to sustain.\n\nThose favouring alternative funding argue that artists, like other professionals, should attract paying customers or private sponsors. Ticket sales, commissions, crowdfunding and corporate partnerships can all finance creative work without placing pressure on public budgets. Market feedback may also encourage artists to communicate clearly with audiences rather than producing work for a narrow circle. Furthermore, government grants can be vulnerable to favouritism, with officials deciding which forms of expression deserve support. Independent funding therefore offers both financial discipline and greater distance from political influence.\n\nNevertheless, market demand alone cannot preserve the full range of a country’s cultural life. Commercial sponsors naturally prefer projects with broad appeal and predictable returns, leaving experimental work, regional traditions and emerging artists at a disadvantage. Public investment can fill this gap by supporting museums, orchestras, community performances and training programmes whose social value exceeds their immediate revenue. For example, grants for traditional musicians can keep rare instruments and performance techniques alive while introducing them to younger audiences. Such support should not guarantee every artist an income; it should be awarded transparently by independent panels and assessed against clear cultural and public-access criteria. Combined with ticket revenue and private sponsorship, this limited intervention would protect diversity without making artists entirely dependent on the state.\n\nIn conclusion, commercial and charitable funding can reward popular, accessible art and reduce demands on taxpayers. However, carefully administered government support remains essential for artistic forms that enrich society but cannot reliably survive in the marketplace.",
