    id: "w-task2-c-09-economic-progress",
    title: "Economic Progress",
    task: "task2",
    type: "discussion",
    topic: "government",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Many governments think that economic progress is their most important goal. Some people, however, think that other types of progress are equally important for a country. Discuss both these views and give your own opinion.</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Governments often treat economic growth as their principal measure of national success, while others argue that social and environmental advances deserve equal attention. A strong economy is indispensable, but I believe progress must be assessed more broadly because income alone does not guarantee a secure or satisfying life.\n\nPrioritising the economy has clear practical advantages. Higher productivity and business activity increase tax revenue, allowing governments to fund hospitals, schools and transport systems. Growth also creates employment, raises household incomes and gives the state greater capacity to respond to crises. For developing countries in particular, expanding basic infrastructure may depend on sustained investment and a larger economic base. Without these resources, ambitious social programmes can become promises that governments cannot finance. Economic progress is therefore a necessary foundation for many other national goals.\n\nHowever, it should remain a means rather than the final objective. Rapid growth can coexist with severe inequality, polluted cities, weak public services and widespread insecurity. If additional wealth is concentrated among a small group or generated by exhausting natural resources, headline figures conceal a decline in ordinary people’s quality of life. Governments should consequently track health, educational attainment, environmental quality and access to affordable housing alongside national income. Costa Rica, for example, has gained international recognition for combining human development with environmental protection rather than pursuing industrial output at any cost. These broader indicators also guide better policy: a government that measures air quality and preventable illness is more likely to invest in clean transport and primary healthcare.\n\nIn conclusion, economic expansion provides jobs and the revenue required for public investment, so it remains essential. Nevertheless, governments should give equal weight to social welfare and environmental resilience, as these determine whether economic gains translate into lasting national progress.",
