    topic: "environment",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Explain some of the ways in which humans are damaging the environment. What can governments do to address these problems? What can individual people do?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Human activity is degrading the natural environment through both large-scale production and everyday consumption. Governments must reshape the systems that cause the greatest harm, while individuals should reduce waste and support the transition through their daily choices.\n\nTwo forms of damage are especially serious. First, burning coal, oil and gas for electricity, transport and industry releases greenhouse gases, which intensify climate change. Second, modern patterns of consumption generate excessive waste and destroy habitats. Forests are cleared for agriculture and construction, while disposable packaging enters rivers and oceans because products are designed for convenience rather than reuse. These pressures reinforce one another: damaged forests absorb less carbon, and rising temperatures further weaken ecosystems. Although individual behaviour contributes to the problem, the scale of the damage is largely determined by energy, transport and production systems.\n\nGovernment action should therefore create conditions in which responsible choices become practical. Authorities can price carbon, phase out the most polluting fuels and invest in reliable public transport and renewable electricity. They should also require manufacturers to reduce packaging, repair products and pay for recycling, shifting responsibility towards those who design the waste. Individuals can then reinforce these policies by using public transport where available, consuming less meat and single-use plastic, and choosing durable goods. Civic behaviour matters equally: voters can support credible environmental policies, and residents can report illegal dumping or join local conservation projects. For example, household waste separation succeeds only when citizens participate and municipalities provide convenient collection facilities. Effective environmental protection thus depends on coordinated roles rather than either side acting alone.\n\nIn conclusion, fossil-fuel use, habitat loss and disposable consumption are closely connected sources of environmental decline. Governments must reform the underlying infrastructure and incentives, while individuals should adopt lower-impact habits and sustain political demand for wider change.",
