    id: "w-task2-c-12-technology",
    title: "Technology",
    task: "task2",
    type: "two-part",
    topic: "technology",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Nowadays the way many people interact with each other has changed because of technology. In what ways has technology affected the types of relationships that people make? Has this been a positive or negative development?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Digital technology has changed not only how people communicate but also whom they are able to know. It has expanded long-distance and interest-based relationships, yet I believe the overall development is positive provided that online contact complements rather than replaces deeper interaction.\n\nPreviously, most relationships developed through family, school, work or the local community. Social platforms and messaging applications have weakened these geographical limits. People can maintain close contact with relatives abroad, collaborate with colleagues whom they have never met, and form friendships around specialised interests that may be uncommon locally. Relationships have also become easier to initiate but often more fluid: a brief exchange can create a connection, while the same connection can disappear without explanation. Online networks therefore include both enduring bonds and large numbers of weak ties. This broader structure can be particularly valuable to migrants, people with disabilities and members of small communities who might otherwise experience isolation.\n\nOn balance, greater access to human connection is beneficial, although its quality requires deliberate attention. Video calls allow families separated by work or study to share ordinary moments, while online groups can provide practical advice and emotional support. During periods of restricted movement, these tools can preserve relationships that physical distance would otherwise erode. However, constant low-effort messaging may create an illusion of closeness, and anonymous platforms can encourage hostility or deception. These risks arise mainly when digital communication substitutes for trust-building experiences rather than supporting them. People should therefore combine online convenience with meaningful conversations and face-to-face contact whenever possible.\n\nIn conclusion, technology has produced more geographically diverse, interest-based and loosely connected relationships. Despite the danger of superficial contact, this is largely a positive development because it enables people to sustain valuable ties and find communities beyond their immediate surroundings.",
