    id: "w-task2-c-13-traditional-cultures-and",
    title: "Traditional Cultures and Technology",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "culture",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>It is inevitable that traditional cultures will be lost as technology develops. Technology and traditional cultures are incompatible. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this view?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "It is sometimes claimed that technological development will inevitably eliminate traditional cultures because the two cannot coexist. I disagree: although technology can accelerate cultural homogenisation, it can also preserve traditions and help them remain meaningful to new generations.\n\nThe concern is understandable because mass media and digital platforms expose people to the same global entertainment, fashion and consumer habits. Younger audiences may consequently regard local customs as old-fashioned, especially when international content is more visible and commercially attractive. Technology can also replace the social settings in which traditions are passed on. If family celebrations become brief online exchanges or crafts are displaced by factory production, practical cultural knowledge may gradually disappear. Nevertheless, this outcome is not caused by technology alone; it reflects choices about education, commercial priorities and how communities use new tools.\n\nUsed deliberately, technology can strengthen rather than weaken cultural continuity. Digital archives can record endangered languages, songs and craft techniques in permanent, accessible forms. Social media also allows traditional performers to reach audiences far beyond a local festival, creating income and renewed interest among younger people. For example, Vietnamese museums and cultural organisations can publish recordings of folk performances alongside explanations of their historical context. This does not freeze culture in the past. Instead, musicians and designers can adapt traditional patterns to contemporary formats while retaining their distinctive origins. Admittedly, an online recording cannot fully reproduce participation in a live ritual, but it can encourage people to attend, learn and continue that practice.\n\nIn conclusion, technology may spread uniform global influences and disrupt some forms of cultural transmission, but incompatibility is not inevitable. With thoughtful education, documentation and creative adaptation, technological tools can give traditional cultures wider visibility and a sustainable place in modern life.",
