    id: "w-task2-c-14-video-games",
    title: "Video Games",
    task: "task2",
    type: "advantages-disadvantages",
    topic: "technology",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Some people regard video games as harmless fun, or even as a useful educational tool. Others, however, believe that video games are having an adverse effect on the people who play them. In your opinion, do the drawbacks of video games outweigh the benefits?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Video games are viewed either as entertainment with educational potential or as a source of serious harm. Although well-designed games can develop useful skills and provide relaxation, I believe their drawbacks outweigh these benefits when play is excessive or shaped by manipulative commercial systems.\n\nThe benefits are genuine but limited. Strategy and puzzle games require players to plan, test solutions and respond to changing information, which can strengthen problem-solving and spatial awareness. Educational games may also make repetitive practice more engaging, especially in language learning or mathematics. Moreover, cooperative online games allow friends to socialise and work towards shared goals. These advantages, however, depend heavily on appropriate content, moderate use and some guidance. Simply spending many hours playing does not automatically produce transferable skills, and most commercial games are designed primarily to retain attention rather than educate.\n\nThe greater concern is that this attention can displace essential activities. Extended play reduces time available for sleep, physical exercise, study and face-to-face relationships, with consequences for both health and performance. Reward systems based on constant upgrades, rankings and daily tasks can make stopping difficult, particularly for younger players whose self-control is still developing. Some games also normalise in-game purchases, encouraging children to spend money for uncertain rewards or competitive advantages. Unlike the possible educational gains, these risks occur across many popular titles and can accumulate unnoticed. Parental time limits, age ratings and stronger regulation of monetisation may reduce the harm, but they cannot remove the commercial incentive to maximise engagement.\n\nIn conclusion, video games can support learning, strategic thinking and social recreation when selected carefully and used in moderation. Nevertheless, their widespread capacity to consume time, weaken healthy routines and encourage manipulative spending means that the disadvantages are more substantial overall.",
