    id: "w-task2-c-15-festivals",
    title: "Festivals",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "culture",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Most people have forgotten the meaning behind traditional or religious festivals; during festival periods, people nowadays only want to enjoy themselves. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this opinion?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Traditional and religious festivals are associated with holidays, shopping and entertainment rather than observance. Although enjoyment has become prominent, I disagree that most people have forgotten what these occasions represent.\n\nAdmittedly, the commercial character of festivals can obscure their purpose. Businesses promote travel packages, special meals and seasonal products, while social media encourages people to focus on decorations and photographs. Consequently, some participants may treat a festival simply as an opportunity to relax without understanding its history. This is particularly evident among children who receive gifts during Christmas or lucky money at Lunar New Year but are not taught why these customs exist. Nevertheless, enjoying a celebration does not necessarily indicate ignorance. Festivals have always included food, music and communal activities, since shared pleasure is one way in which cultural traditions remain appealing and are passed from one generation to the next.\n\nMore importantly, many families and communities still preserve the deeper meaning of these events through regular practices. During Tet in Vietnam, for example, people commonly visit relatives, remember deceased family members and express hopes for a peaceful year. These activities reflect respect for family ties and ancestors, even when they are accompanied by parties or tourism. Religious communities likewise continue to hold services, processions and charitable events that explain the moral significance of holy days. Modern forms of celebration may therefore change the outward appearance of a festival without removing its central values. Indeed, entertainment can attract younger people, after which families, schools and religious institutions have an opportunity to explain the tradition behind it.\n\nIn conclusion, commercialisation has undoubtedly caused some people to engage with festivals superficially. However, enjoyment and cultural understanding can coexist, and the continued practice of family, religious and charitable customs shows that the meaning of festivals has not been forgotten by most people.",
