    id: "w-task2-c-16-high-salaries",
    title: "High Salaries",
    task: "task2",
    type: "discussion",
    topic: "employment",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>In many countries, a small number of people earn extremely high salaries. Some people believe that this is good for the country, but others think that governments should not allow salaries above a certain level. Discuss both these views and give your own opinion.</p>",
    modelAnswer: "The exceptionally high salaries earned by a small minority have prompted debate about whether governments should impose an upper limit on pay. Although a salary cap may appear to promote fairness, I believe high earnings should remain legal because they can reward valuable work and encourage economic activity.\n\nOn the one hand, limiting salaries could reduce excessive inequality. When senior executives receive hundreds of times more than ordinary employees, workplace morale may decline because staff feel that their contribution is undervalued. A legal ceiling could also allow companies to redirect money towards wages, training or public taxes rather than concentrating it among a few individuals. However, a universal cap would be difficult to set fairly. A level considered excessive in one industry or city might be reasonable elsewhere, and companies could avoid the rule by offering shares or benefits instead of cash.\n\nOn the other hand, very high pay can serve legitimate economic purposes. It enables firms to attract people whose decisions carry unusual responsibility, such as surgeons, technology specialists or leaders of large organisations. If their potential reward were restricted, some might move to countries with more competitive labour markets, taking expertise and investment with them. High earners also pay substantial income tax where taxation is progressive, thereby helping to fund public services. More fundamentally, governments should address inequality through the tax system rather than controlling private contracts. For example, higher tax bands and effective rules against avoidance can return part of exceptional incomes to society while preserving incentives for innovation and demanding work.\n\nIn conclusion, salary limits might narrow visible pay gaps, but they would be inflexible and relatively easy to bypass. Allowing high salaries, combined with progressive taxation and stronger protection for lower-paid workers, is a more practical way to balance economic incentives with social fairness.",
