    id: "w-task2-c-17-climate-change",
    title: "Climate Change",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "environment",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Some people think that instead of preventing climate change, we need to find a way to live with it. To what extent do you agree or disagree?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "As the effects of climate change become increasingly visible, some argue that societies should adapt to them rather than attempt to prevent further warming. While adaptation is now essential, I strongly disagree that it should replace efforts to reduce greenhouse-gas emissions.\n\nIt is true that a degree of climate change is already unavoidable, so communities must prepare for its consequences. Coastal cities need stronger flood defences, while farming regions may require drought-resistant crops and more efficient irrigation. Such measures protect lives and livelihoods from risks that cannot be eliminated immediately. For example, heatwave warning systems can help hospitals and local authorities support elderly residents before temperatures become dangerous. Nevertheless, adaptation has practical limits. Poorer countries cannot continually rebuild homes and infrastructure after severe storms, and certain losses, including the disappearance of low-lying islands or ecosystems, cannot be reversed through better planning.\n\nPreventing further change therefore remains the more fundamental response. If governments reduce dependence on fossil fuels, improve energy efficiency and protect forests, they can slow the accumulation of emissions that intensifies extreme weather. This addresses the source of the problem rather than repeatedly paying for its consequences. It also brings immediate benefits: cleaner transport reduces urban air pollution, and renewable energy can make countries less vulnerable to unstable fuel prices. Crucially, mitigation and adaptation reinforce one another. Every fraction of warming avoided reduces the scale and cost of the protective measures that future generations will need, making it more realistic for societies to cope with the remaining effects.\n\nIn conclusion, living with some consequences of climate change is unavoidable and requires serious investment. However, treating adaptation as an alternative to prevention would allow the underlying threat to grow beyond manageable limits; both approaches are necessary, with emission reduction remaining the priority.",
