    id: "w-task2-c-18-city-life",
    title: "City Life",
    task: "task2",
    type: "problem-solution",
    topic: "cities",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>More and more people are migrating to cities in search of a better life, but city life can be extremely difficult. Explain some of the difficulties of living in a city. How can governments make urban life better for everyone?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Cities continue to attract people seeking employment, education and improved services, yet rapid population growth often makes urban life difficult. The main pressures involve housing and transport, and governments can ease them through better planning and sustained public investment.\n\nThe first difficulty is the cost of accommodation. When demand rises faster than supply, rents consume a large share of household income, forcing low-paid workers into overcrowded rooms or distant suburbs. This creates a second burden: long and unreliable journeys. Roads become congested as commuters travel from affordable areas to central workplaces, while packed public transport reduces comfort and productivity. In cities such as Ho Chi Minh City, these pressures are connected. A family may find cheaper housing on the outskirts, but the resulting commute costs time, fuel and opportunities to care for children. Pollution, noise and limited access to green space further weaken residents' health.\n\nGovernments should therefore coordinate housing, transport and neighbourhood services. They can require large developments to include affordable homes and use public land for social housing near employment centres. At the same time, reliable metro and bus networks would allow people to reach work without depending on private vehicles, reducing both congestion and air pollution. Urban improvement must also extend beyond wealthy central districts. Building clinics, schools, parks and drainage systems in underserved neighbourhoods would distribute essential services more evenly and reduce the need for daily cross-city travel. These measures work best together: affordable housing is far less useful if it remains disconnected from jobs, while transport investment alone cannot compensate for unsafe living conditions.\n\nIn conclusion, city residents commonly face unaffordable housing, exhausting travel and an unhealthy environment. Integrated policies that connect reasonably priced homes with efficient transport and accessible local services would make urban opportunities available to a much wider population.",
