    id: "w-task2-c-19-live-alone",
    title: "Live Alone",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "society",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>In some countries, many more people are choosing to live alone nowadays than in the past. Do you think this is a positive or negative development?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Living alone has become common as incomes, housing options and social expectations have changed. Although this arrangement offers independence, I believe its growth is negative because it can increase isolation and pressure housing and public services.\n\nAdmittedly, a one-person household allows individuals to organise life according to their own priorities. Young adults can pursue work or study without family restrictions, while people leaving an unhappy relationship have a realistic alternative to remaining dependent on a partner. Managing rent, meals and household decisions may also build confidence and practical skills. These benefits are particularly important for women in societies where earlier generations had limited control over where and how they lived. However, independence does not require permanent separation from others, and many of these advantages can also be achieved in a respectful shared household.\n\nThe larger concern is that widespread solo living weakens both personal support networks and the efficient use of resources. A person who becomes ill or unemployed has no one at home to notice the problem or share immediate responsibilities, which can turn temporary difficulty into prolonged loneliness. At a societal level, one-person households require more dwellings, appliances and energy per resident than shared homes. This raises demand in cities where affordable housing is already scarce. An ageing population makes the pattern even more costly, since older adults living alone may depend heavily on professional carers and emergency services for assistance that relatives once provided informally. The issue is not that every individual household is harmful, but that a large-scale shift can leave communities more fragmented and expensive to support.\n\nIn conclusion, living alone can provide autonomy and safety for particular individuals. Nevertheless, its increasing prevalence is negative overall because the resulting social isolation and inefficient use of housing and care resources outweigh the personal convenience it offers.",
