    id: "w-task2-c-20-crime",
    title: "Crime",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "crime",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Some people who have been in prison become good citizens later, and it is often argued that these are the best people to talk to teenagers about the dangers of committing a crime. To what extent do you agree or disagree?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Former prisoners who have rebuilt their lives are sometimes invited to warn teenagers about crime. I agree that they can be effective speakers, although they should contribute to a broader prevention programme rather than be regarded as automatically the best source of guidance.\n\nTheir main strength is credibility based on direct experience. A police officer or teacher can explain that crime leads to punishment, but a former offender can describe the reality of losing freedom, damaging family relationships and struggling to find work after release. This detail makes the consequences concrete for teenagers who may otherwise view offending as exciting or low-risk. A reformed citizen can also explain the decisions that preceded a serious offence, helping young people recognise peer pressure and withdraw before a situation escalates. For example, an account of how carrying a weapon for protection led to violence may challenge the belief that such behaviour increases personal safety.\n\nNevertheless, lived experience alone does not make someone a suitable educator. Some former prisoners may glamorise their past, offer an unrepresentative story or lack the skills to respond responsibly to vulnerable students. Schools should therefore select speakers carefully, prepare sessions with counsellors and avoid presenting one biography as a universal path into crime. Other professionals remain essential: police can explain legal consequences, psychologists can address aggression or addiction, and youth workers can connect teenagers with constructive activities. When these perspectives are combined, the former offender supplies emotional impact while qualified staff provide reliable information and continuing support. This is more effective than a speech with no follow-up.\n\nIn conclusion, reformed prisoners can deliver uniquely persuasive warnings because they understand both the attraction and the real cost of crime. However, they are most valuable as carefully selected partners within a professionally designed education programme, not as replacements for trained specialists.",
    wordCount: 300,
