    id: "w-task2-c-22-foreign-films",
    title: "Foreign Films",
    task: "task2",
    type: "two-part",
    topic: "film",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Many people prefer to watch foreign films rather than locally produced films. Why could this be? Should governments give more financial support to local film industries?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Foreign films attract larger audiences than domestic productions in many countries. This preference reflects differences in quality and variety, and I believe governments should support local cinema where funding is tied to cultural and commercial development.\n\nMajor international studios generally possess budgets that smaller national industries cannot match. They can employ recognised actors, sophisticated visual effects and extensive marketing, making their releases appear more polished and easier to discover. Global distribution also gives viewers access to a wider range of genres than a limited local market may sustain. For example, audiences interested in large-scale science fiction are likely to choose Hollywood or Korean productions if domestic companies mainly produce romantic comedies. In addition, streaming platforms frequently recommend content that has already succeeded internationally. This creates a cycle in which foreign films gain greater visibility, attract more viewers and receive still more promotion, while local work struggles to reach an audience.\n\nPublic support is therefore justified, but it should build capacity rather than protect weak films indefinitely. Grants can train screenwriters and technicians, preserve stories in national languages and enable new directors to complete projects that private investors consider risky. Governments can also fund film schools, regional cinemas and international festival participation, giving the entire industry stronger foundations. However, subsidies should be awarded through independent competition and accompanied by clear expectations for quality and audience development. Simply paying studios to produce more films could encourage dependency and waste public money. A balanced scheme would provide early support while requiring successful producers to attract private investment and distribute their work effectively.\n\nIn conclusion, foreign films often dominate because they combine greater resources, broader choice and superior visibility. Carefully designed government assistance can help local industries close this gap and preserve cultural expression, provided that funding rewards potential and encourages long-term self-sufficiency.",
