    id: "w-task2-c-23-road-safety",
    title: "Road Safety",
    task: "task2",
    type: "discussion",
    topic: "transport",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Some people think that strict punishments for driving offences are the key to reducing traffic accidents. Others, however, believe that other measures would be more effective in improving road safety. Discuss both these views and give your own opinion.</p>",
    modelAnswer: "There is debate over whether severe penalties or other measures best prevent road accidents. Although punishment is necessary to deter dangerous driving, I believe safer infrastructure and consistent enforcement have greater impact.\n\nOn the one hand, strict penalties can change behaviour by making offences costly. Large fines, licence suspension and imprisonment give drivers a strong reason to avoid speeding, drink-driving and mobile-phone use. Removing repeat offenders from the road also protects other users directly. However, severity alone has limited value if drivers believe they are unlikely to be caught. An extreme penalty imposed after only a small proportion of offences may deter less effectively than a moderate but predictable consequence. Excessively harsh rules can also punish an isolated mistake in the same way as deliberate, repeated risk-taking unless courts retain discretion.\n\nOn the other hand, road safety depends greatly on the environment in which decisions are made. Governments can redesign dangerous junctions, provide protected lanes for motorcycles and bicycles, improve street lighting and separate pedestrians from fast-moving traffic. These changes prevent collisions even when a driver makes an error. At the same time, visible traffic police, speed cameras and reliable vehicle inspections increase the certainty that unsafe conduct will be detected. Education also has a role when it is practical: learner drivers should receive supervised road experience rather than memorise rules solely to pass a test. For example, clearly marked crossings combined with lower speed limits near schools protect children every day, whereas punishment only operates after someone has broken the law.\n\nIn conclusion, strict punishments are an important part of road safety because serious and repeated offences require firm consequences. Nevertheless, well-designed roads, dependable enforcement and better driver training are more effective because they reduce both the opportunity for dangerous behaviour and the damage caused by human error.",
