    id: "w-task2-c-24-parental-roles",
    title: "Parental Roles",
    task: "task2",
    type: "two-part",
    topic: "family",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>These days more fathers stay at home and take care of their children while mothers go out to work. What could be the reasons for this? Do you think it is a positive or a negative development?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "More families have a father providing childcare while the mother works. This reflects women's stronger career prospects and flexible ideas about parenthood, and I consider it a positive development.\n\nEconomic and social changes both explain the reversal of conventional roles. Women now attain high levels of education and enter professions in which their income or promotion prospects may exceed those of their partners. If childcare is expensive, it is rational for the lower earner to pause employment, regardless of gender. Meanwhile, remote and freelance work allows some fathers to combine paid tasks with school runs and household duties. Attitudes have also shifted: caring for children is increasingly viewed as a parental responsibility rather than work naturally assigned to mothers. Paternity leave and visible examples of involved fathers make this choice more acceptable, enabling couples to select roles according to their circumstances instead of social expectations.\n\nThis development benefits both families and society. Children can form a close bond with their fathers and learn that care, patience and domestic competence are not gender-specific qualities. Mothers, in turn, can maintain professional experience and financial independence rather than losing years of career progression after childbirth. The arrangement may also improve relationships because each parent gains a clearer understanding of both earning and caring responsibilities. Admittedly, a stay-at-home father may encounter social judgement or difficulty returning to work later, but these are consequences of outdated attitudes and weak employment protection, not flaws in the family model itself. As the pattern becomes more familiar, such disadvantages should diminish.\n\nIn conclusion, fathers increasingly become primary carers because couples respond pragmatically to income differences, flexible work and changing beliefs about gender. This is a positive shift, as it expands parental choice, strengthens father-child relationships and allows mothers to pursue careers without neglecting family needs.",
