    id: "w-task2-c-25-wild-animals",
    title: "Wild Animals",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "environment",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Wild animals have no place in the 21st century, so protecting them is a waste of resources. To what extent do you agree or disagree?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "As human societies become increasingly urbanised, some people argue that wild animals are no longer relevant and that spending money on their protection is wasteful. I completely disagree because wildlife supports essential natural systems, while conservation also brings substantial economic and scientific benefits.\n\nThe strongest reason for protecting wild species is that they maintain ecosystems on which people ultimately depend. Predators control populations of smaller animals, insects pollinate crops, and forests containing diverse species regulate water and store carbon. Removing even one species can therefore trigger a chain reaction that damages agriculture or increases the risk of flooding and disease. Protecting wolves, for example, has helped prevent deer from overgrazing vegetation beside rivers, allowing these habitats to recover. Conservation funding is consequently not a sentimental expense devoted only to animals; it is a practical investment in food security, clean water and a stable climate. These services would be extremely costly for technology to replace.\n\nWildlife can also create direct value for modern societies. Well-managed national parks attract visitors, support local employment and give rural communities a financial reason to preserve habitats rather than clear them. In addition, wild plants and animals provide scientists with genetic material and biological processes that may contribute to new medicines or more resilient crops. Admittedly, governments facing poverty cannot devote unlimited funds to every endangered species. However, this calls for careful prioritisation, not the abandonment of conservation. Resources can be directed towards habitats that protect numerous species at once, while tourism revenue and international partnerships can share the cost.\n\nIn conclusion, wild animals still have an important place in the modern world because healthy ecosystems sustain human life and conservation produces economic and scientific returns. Although expenditure should be targeted carefully, treating all wildlife protection as wasteful would be both short-sighted and ultimately expensive.",
