    id: "w-task2-c-26-happiness",
    title: "Happiness",
    task: "task2",
    type: "two-part",
    topic: "society",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Happiness is considered very important in life. Why is it difficult to define? What factors are important in achieving happiness?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Although happiness is widely regarded as a central aim in life, there is little agreement about what it actually means. It is difficult to define because it is subjective and changes over time, while its achievement usually depends on a combination of secure living conditions, meaningful relationships and a sense of purpose.\n\nNo single definition can capture happiness because people evaluate their lives according to different values and circumstances. One person may associate it with professional achievement, whereas another may value family time or personal freedom more highly. Furthermore, an individual's definition can change across different stages of life. A young graduate may be delighted by independence and career progress, but later place greater importance on health and time with children. Happiness is therefore not one permanent emotional state or a fixed list of possessions, but a changing judgement about whether life matches one's present needs.\n\nNevertheless, several broad conditions make happiness more attainable. Basic financial security is important because people who can afford housing, healthcare and daily necessities are less exposed to persistent anxiety. Beyond this threshold, however, relationships often matter more than further income. Supportive relatives and friends provide companionship as well as practical help during illness, unemployment or other difficulties. A sense of purpose is equally significant. Meaningful work, raising a family, volunteering or developing a skill gives people evidence that their time has value. Finally, physical and mental health enable them to enjoy these advantages; without adequate rest or access to care, even material success can feel empty.\n\nIn conclusion, happiness resists a universal definition because values vary among individuals, cultures and life stages. Even so, people are generally more likely to achieve it when material stability is combined with close social bonds, worthwhile goals and good health.",
