    id: "w-task2-c-27-salary",
    title: "Salary",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "work",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>When choosing a job, the salary is the most important consideration. To what extent do you agree or disagree?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Income inevitably influences people's career choices because employment must provide a reasonable standard of living. However, I disagree that salary is always the most important consideration, as long-term job satisfaction and sustainable working conditions usually have a greater effect on an employee's overall quality of life.\n\nAdmittedly, pay deserves considerable attention, especially for workers with financial responsibilities. A sufficient salary allows people to cover housing, healthcare and education, while also building savings for emergencies. If two positions offer similar duties and prospects, choosing the better-paid one is entirely rational. Salary can also signal how much an organisation values a person's expertise, so persistent underpayment may reduce motivation. For employees in expensive cities, accepting enjoyable but poorly paid work may simply be unrealistic. In such circumstances, income is not a superficial attraction but a prerequisite for stability and independence.\n\nNevertheless, once basic needs are met, other features often determine whether a career remains worthwhile. Excessive workloads, insecure contracts or an abusive manager can damage mental and physical health regardless of the salary offered. By contrast, employees who have supportive colleagues, reasonable hours and opportunities to develop are more likely to remain engaged and progress professionally. A software engineer who earns slightly less but receives training and flexible hours, for instance, may acquire stronger skills and avoid burnout, eventually achieving both higher earnings and greater satisfaction. Moreover, work occupies a substantial part of adult life. Choosing solely according to pay can therefore trap people in roles that conflict with their abilities or values, making resignation and repeated career changes more likely.\n\nIn conclusion, salary is an essential factor when it provides security and fairly reflects an employee's contribution. Even so, I believe that healthy working conditions, personal fulfilment and opportunities for growth are more important once a reasonable income has been secured.",
