    id: "w-task2-c-29-foreign-tourists",
    title: "Foreign Tourists",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "tourism",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Foreign visitors should pay more than local visitors for cultural and historical attractions. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this opinion?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Some cultural and historical attractions charge international tourists more than domestic visitors. I largely agree with this policy because local people already support these sites through taxation and lower resident prices can preserve public access, provided that the difference remains reasonable and transparent.\n\nCharging foreign visitors a modest premium can distribute the cost of preservation more fairly. Museums, monuments and archaeological sites require security, restoration and specialist staff, yet entrance fees rarely cover all these expenses. Local residents commonly contribute through national or municipal taxes whether or not they visit, whereas short-term tourists make no equivalent contribution. A higher ticket price therefore asks them to bear a fairer share of the maintenance costs. At heavily visited sites such as ancient temples, the additional revenue can fund repairs and visitor management, protecting the attraction from the pressure created partly by tourism itself.\n\nReduced prices for residents also serve an important educational and cultural purpose. Historical places form part of a community's shared heritage, so high uniform fees could exclude the very people whose history is being presented. Affordable access enables school groups and families to visit regularly, strengthening public understanding of local culture. Nevertheless, extreme price gaps would be difficult to justify. They may make foreigners feel exploited, damage a destination's reputation and discourage budget travellers from visiting less famous sites. Authorities should therefore publish a clear resident discount rather than an arbitrary tourist surcharge, and the extra income should visibly support conservation. Free days or concessions could also protect access for foreign students and other visitors with limited means.\n\nIn conclusion, I support moderately higher admission charges for foreign tourists because residents contribute through taxes and deserve affordable access to their own heritage. However, such pricing should remain proportionate, clearly explained and directly connected to preservation.",
