    id: "w-task2-c-30-ageing-population",
    title: "Ageing Population",
    task: "task2",
    type: "problem-solution",
    topic: "society",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>In the developed world, average life expectancy is increasing. What problems will this cause for individuals and society? Suggest some measures that could be taken to reduce the impact of ageing populations.</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Longer life expectancy is a major achievement, but it also creates difficulties when the proportion of elderly people rises faster than the working population. This trend can place financial and caring pressures on individuals and public services, although flexible retirement, preventive healthcare and support for carers can reduce its impact.\n\nAgeing populations create several connected problems. Governments must fund pensions and treat more age-related conditions while collecting taxes from a relatively smaller workforce. Hospitals may consequently face longer waiting times, and spending on elderly care can limit investment in education or infrastructure. Families are affected as well. Middle-aged adults may need to support both children and ageing parents, reducing their income and sometimes forcing them to leave employment. For older individuals, living longer does not necessarily mean living well: poor health, loneliness and inadequate savings can make an extended retirement insecure. These pressures reinforce one another, since relatives who become unpaid carers contribute less tax precisely when public expenditure is rising.\n\nThe impact can be reduced through measures that keep people healthy, economically active and supported. Governments should permit gradual retirement and encourage employers to offer part-time or less physically demanding roles to older workers who wish to continue. Greater investment in screening, exercise programmes and community healthcare could also prevent expensive chronic illnesses. Finally, carers need practical assistance through respite services, paid leave and affordable home care. Japan, for example, has expanded community-based services and technology that help older people remain at home, reducing pressure on hospitals and relatives. Managed immigration can supplement these policies where labour shortages remain.\n\nIn conclusion, population ageing may strain public finances, healthcare and family life while exposing elderly people to isolation and insecurity. A combination of flexible employment, preventive medicine and stronger care systems can make longer lives more sustainable for all generations.",
