    id: "w-task2-c-31-animal-testing",
    title: "Animal Testing",
    task: "task2",
    type: "discussion",
    topic: "science",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Nowadays animal experiments are widely used to develop new medicines and to test the safety of other products. Some people argue that these experiments should be banned because it is morally wrong to cause animals to suffer, while others are in favour of them because of their benefits to humanity. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Animal experiments remain common, creating a conflict between animal welfare and human benefit. Although the suffering involved gives strong grounds for restricting such research, I believe carefully regulated experiments should remain available for essential medical purposes when no reliable alternative exists.\n\nOn the one hand, opponents argue that animals should not be made to suffer for human convenience. Laboratory procedures may cause pain, fear or confinement, yet animals cannot consent or escape. This is especially difficult to justify when experiments are used for non-essential cosmetics or repeat tests whose risks are already understood. Moreover, results from one species do not always predict human responses accurately, meaning that suffering may produce limited value. A complete ban would force companies to invest more quickly in alternatives such as cell cultures, computer models and artificial human tissue, which can sometimes be both more ethical and more precise.\n\nOn the other hand, a prohibition could obstruct the development of treatments that protect millions of people. Before a new drug is given to volunteers, researchers must understand how it affects an entire living system, including organs and reproduction; laboratory alternatives cannot yet reproduce every interaction. Animal studies have therefore contributed to vaccines, surgical techniques and medicines for serious diseases. They can also benefit animals through veterinary treatments. In my view, this potential justifies limited testing only under strict conditions. Regulators should require researchers to prove that no alternative method is adequate, use the fewest animals possible and minimise pain through appropriate care. Tests for cosmetics and other non-essential goods should be banned, while medical research should face independent review and public reporting.\n\nIn conclusion, the moral objections to animal testing are valid and demand far tighter limits. Nevertheless, I favour retaining rigorously controlled experiments for indispensable medical research until equally reliable alternatives can replace them.",
