    type: "opinion",
    topic: "society",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Some people think that all teenagers should be required to do unpaid work in their free time to help the local community. They believe this would benefit both the individual teenager and society as a whole. Do you agree or disagree?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Requiring every teenager to undertake unpaid community work is often presented as a way to develop responsible citizens and improve local services. I agree that structured volunteering should form part of secondary education, provided that the commitment is limited, flexible and designed around genuine community needs.\n\nCommunity work can teach lessons that classrooms cannot provide as directly. By helping at a library, food bank or neighbourhood clean-up, teenagers learn to communicate with people outside their usual social group and see the practical consequences of cooperation. These experiences can strengthen confidence and reveal interests that influence later study or employment. At the same time, communities gain help for useful but labour-intensive activities. For example, teenagers could assist older residents with digital services, giving those residents greater independence while allowing students to apply everyday technological skills in a meaningful context.\n\nNevertheless, a requirement can become unfair or ineffective if it ignores individual circumstances. Some teenagers already care for relatives, work to support their households or travel long distances to school. Demanding many additional hours would penalise them and could damage their education. Compulsory programmes may also produce reluctant participants who are given artificial tasks so that schools can record attendance. To avoid these problems, schools should include a modest number of service hours within the timetable rather than taking unrestricted amounts of free time. Students should be offered several roles, including remote or school-based options, and existing caring responsibilities should count towards the requirement. Supervision and reflection would ensure that the experience remains educational instead of becoming a source of free labour.\n\nIn conclusion, I support a carefully designed community-service requirement because it can build practical skills and address local needs. However, its benefits depend on flexible participation, reasonable hours and meaningful placements that recognise teenagers' differing responsibilities.",
