    id: "w-task2-c-33-hobbies",
    title: "Hobbies",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "leisure",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Some people believe that hobbies need to be difficult to be enjoyable. To what extent do you agree or disagree?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "It is sometimes claimed that leisure activities are enjoyable only when they involve considerable difficulty. While challenge can make a hobby deeply satisfying for some people, I disagree that it is necessary, because enjoyment can equally arise from relaxation, creativity and social connection.\n\nDifficult hobbies can certainly provide a distinctive form of pleasure. Activities such as learning a musical instrument, rock climbing or mastering a complex strategy game require sustained effort, so each improvement feels earned. The gap between a person's initial ability and eventual competence creates visible progress, which can strengthen confidence and maintain motivation over many years. Challenge also demands concentration. When participants focus completely on a demanding movement or problem, they temporarily stop thinking about work and other pressures. However, these benefits depend on the level of difficulty remaining manageable; repeated failure without guidance is more likely to produce frustration than enjoyment.\n\nMany worthwhile hobbies are pleasurable for entirely different reasons. Gardening, casual photography, walking or listening to music can offer recovery from demanding jobs and family responsibilities. Their value lies precisely in the absence of pressure, targets and evaluation. Simple activities can also encourage creativity, since participants are free to experiment without fearing poor performance. In addition, the social setting may matter more than the task itself. A weekly cooking group or informal football match can be enjoyable because it creates regular contact and shared memories, even when nobody is trying to reach an advanced standard. Since people have different temperaments and amounts of free time, requiring difficulty as a condition of enjoyment imposes one narrow preference on a highly varied part of life.\n\nIn conclusion, challenging hobbies can be rewarding because they offer progress, mastery and intense concentration. Nevertheless, difficulty is not essential: relaxation, creative freedom and companionship provide equally valid sources of enjoyment.",
