    id: "w-task2-c-34-help-people-in-other-countri",
    title: "Help People in Other Countries",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "society",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>We cannot help everyone in the world that needs help, so we should only be concerned with our own communities and countries. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this statement?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Since resources are limited, no government or individual can respond to every person in need. However, I strongly disagree that concern should stop at national borders, because severe suffering creates a moral case for assistance and many international problems eventually affect domestic communities.\n\nIt is reasonable for governments to give priority to their own citizens. They collect taxes to provide local healthcare, education, security and infrastructure, and neglecting these duties would weaken public trust. Assistance is also often more effective close to home because local organisations understand the language, institutions and needs of the people they serve. Nevertheless, prioritising domestic obligations is different from accepting exclusive responsibility for them. A wealthy country can maintain essential public services while devoting a controlled share of its budget to urgent crises elsewhere.\n\nInternational assistance is justified both ethically and practically. When people face famine, war or natural disaster, their need for food, shelter and medicine matters more than their nationality. Modest contributions from prosperous societies can save lives where local governments lack the necessary resources. Furthermore, modern countries are deeply connected. Uncontrolled disease outbreaks can cross borders, conflicts can displace large populations, and environmental damage can affect food supplies and weather patterns far away. Helping another country strengthen hospitals, accommodate refugees or adapt to drought may therefore prevent a larger and more expensive crisis later. This support need not be unlimited or poorly monitored. Governments can focus on emergencies where aid has measurable value, work through accountable organisations and coordinate contributions so that responsibility is shared rather than carried by a few donors.\n\nIn conclusion, countries must meet their responsibilities at home, but this does not justify ignoring serious need abroad. Targeted international aid is both a humane response to extreme suffering and a sensible investment in the stability of an interconnected world.",
