    id: "w-task2-c-35-museums",
    title: "Museums",
    task: "task2",
    type: "discussion",
    topic: "museums",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Some people think that museums should be enjoyable places to entertain people, while others believe that the purpose of museums is to educate. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.</p>",
    modelAnswer: "People disagree over whether museums should primarily entertain visitors or educate them. Although an enjoyable experience can attract a wider audience, I believe education should remain their central purpose because museums preserve knowledge that few other institutions can provide so directly.\n\nOn the one hand, making museums entertaining can increase public engagement. Traditional displays consisting of objects behind glass and lengthy descriptions may seem uninviting, particularly to children and visitors with little prior knowledge. Interactive exhibits, reconstructed historical settings and digital games can turn a passive visit into a memorable experience. This wider appeal also strengthens museums financially: larger audiences generate more ticket and shop revenue, which can fund conservation. For example, a science museum that allows children to operate simple machines may encourage families to return rather than treating the visit as a compulsory school activity.\n\nOn the other hand, entertainment should support, rather than replace, serious learning. Museums hold authentic objects that connect abstract accounts of history, science and culture to physical evidence. When these items are carefully explained, visitors can understand not only what happened but also why it mattered. This role is especially important when commercial media reduces complex events to dramatic stories designed to hold attention. If museums followed the same approach, uncomfortable facts might be simplified or omitted because they were not enjoyable. A war museum, for instance, has a responsibility to present civilian suffering accurately, even if the experience is disturbing. Well-designed storytelling and technology can make such material accessible, but their value lies in clarifying the evidence, not distracting from it.\n\nIn conclusion, entertaining displays help museums overcome barriers to participation and remain financially viable. Nevertheless, education must guide their choices, with enjoyable methods serving as a means of making reliable knowledge more engaging rather than becoming an end in themselves.",
