    id: "w-task2-c-36-career-pathway",
    title: "Career Pathway",
    task: "task2",
    type: "discussion",
    topic: "education and careers",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Some people believe that studying at university or college is the best route to a successful career, while others believe that it is better to get a job straight after school. Discuss both views and give your opinion.</p>",
    modelAnswer: "There is disagreement about whether school-leavers should pursue higher education or enter employment immediately. Starting work early offers practical benefits, but I believe university or college is generally the stronger route because it provides specialised knowledge and broader long-term opportunities.\n\nOn the one hand, taking a job after school allows young people to gain experience while becoming financially independent. Instead of paying tuition fees, they earn an income and learn workplace skills such as meeting deadlines and cooperating with colleagues. Early employment can be particularly effective in occupations where competence is developed mainly through practice. An apprentice electrician, for example, may progress faster by working alongside an experienced professional than by spending several years studying unrelated academic subjects. Such workers also begin building professional contacts earlier, which can lead to promotion or help them establish their own businesses.\n\nOn the other hand, tertiary education remains a prerequisite for many rewarding careers. Doctors, engineers and teachers require systematic training because mistakes in these professions can have serious consequences. Beyond providing credentials, higher education develops analytical skills by requiring students to assess evidence, solve unfamiliar problems and defend their conclusions. These abilities make graduates more adaptable when technology changes roles. While an entry-level worker may initially earn more than a student, limited qualifications can later prevent that person from moving into specialised or managerial positions. College also gives uncertain school-leavers time to explore related fields through courses, placements and contact with professionals before committing to one pathway. It therefore offers not merely a first job, but a foundation for sustained career development.\n\nIn conclusion, immediate employment is a sensible option for practical trades and gives young people an early financial advantage. Nevertheless, for most professional careers, higher education provides the expertise, flexibility and recognised qualifications needed for success over the longer term.",
