    id: "w-task2-c-38-permissive-parenting",
    title: "Permissive Parenting",
    task: "task2",
    type: "two-part",
    topic: "parenting",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Some parents buy their children whatever they ask for, and allow their children to do whatever they want. Is this a good way to raise children? What consequences could this style of parenting have for children as they get older?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Some parents satisfy every request their children make and impose almost no limits on their behaviour. This is not an effective way to raise children because it prevents them from learning self-control, and it may produce adults who struggle with responsibility, relationships and disappointment.\n\nChildren need warmth and freedom, but unlimited choice gives them no opportunity to understand boundaries. When parents buy a desired toy or permit inappropriate behaviour to avoid conflict, the child learns that demands are rewarded. This removes chances to practise waiting, prioritising needs and accepting that other people also have rights. Clear rules, by contrast, connect actions with consequences and clarify what is expected. Refusing an unnecessary purchase, for example, can lead to a useful conversation about saving money rather than being unkind. Effective parenting therefore combines affection with consistent limits instead of treating every restriction as harmful.\n\nAs permissively raised children grow older, habits formed at home can become serious disadvantages. They may manage money poorly because they have never distinguished between wanting something and being able to afford it. In education and employment, they can find criticism, deadlines and delayed rewards unusually difficult, which may cause them to abandon demanding goals. The social consequences are significant. Someone accustomed to having personal wishes placed first may struggle to compromise with friends, colleagues or a partner, creating repeated conflict. Most importantly, adult institutions will not adjust to their preferences as parents once did. The resulting gap between expectation and reality can bring frustration, low resilience and dependence on family support long after independence should have developed.\n\nIn conclusion, granting children every request is not generous parenting but a failure to prepare them for limits. Without firm yet reasonable boundaries, they risk entering adulthood without the financial discipline, perseverance and mutual consideration required for an independent life.",
