    id: "w-task2-c-40-hobbies",
    title: "Hobbies",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "hobbies",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Popular hobbies and interests change over time and are more a reflection of trends and fashions than an indication of what individuals really want to do in their spare time. To what extent do you agree or disagree?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "The popularity of leisure activities shifts considerably over time, leading some to argue that hobbies reflect fashion rather than genuine personal preference. I partly disagree: trends strongly influence which activities people discover, but lasting participation usually depends on authentic enjoyment or personal value.\n\nSocial forces clearly shape leisure choices. Online platforms expose users to activities, from home baking to running clubs, and attractive images can make participation seem desirable. Businesses reinforce this effect by marketing equipment and classes around each trend. People may try a hobby because friends are doing it or because displaying it supports a fashionable identity. During a promoted fitness challenge, for example, many individuals may buy specialist clothing and post their progress despite little previous interest in exercise. When public attention moves elsewhere, these participants often stop, suggesting that their involvement was driven more by visibility than by deep preference.\n\nHowever, fashion cannot fully explain why individuals devote free time to an activity. A trend may lower the barrier to entry by providing information, affordable equipment and a community, but the person still experiences the hobby directly. Those who find satisfaction, relaxation or social connection are likely to continue after its popularity fades. Moreover, preferences are not necessarily fixed before exposure. Someone who joins a fashionable pottery class may discover genuine pleasure in making useful objects by hand. In this case, the trend has revealed rather than replaced an individual interest. Less visible hobbies such as reading, gardening and fishing persist across generations, demonstrating that many choices meet enduring psychological needs regardless of fashion.\n\nIn conclusion, trends affect the hobbies people notice and may create short-lived waves of participation. Nevertheless, they do not determine all leisure behaviour, since sustained involvement depends largely on whether an activity provides enjoyment, meaning or worthwhile relationships for the individual.",
