    type: "opinion",
    topic: "consumer choice",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Some people believe that nowadays we have too many choices. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this statement?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Modern consumers can choose among an unprecedented range of products, services and life pathways. I agree that this abundance has become excessive in many areas because comparing near-identical options consumes attention and can reduce satisfaction, although meaningful choice remains essential.\n\nAn extensive range is beneficial when alternatives meet genuinely different needs. Students, for instance, can select courses suited to varied abilities and career plans, while patients may choose treatments according to effectiveness, cost and side effects. Competition between providers can also improve quality because people are free to reject poor service. Removing such choices would force diverse individuals into the same arrangement and give powerful institutions little reason to respond to users. The problem, therefore, is not choice itself but the multiplication of options that differ only superficially.\n\nIn many markets, this multiplication creates more burden than freedom. A shopper selecting a household product may face dozens of versions distinguished by minor features and promotional language. Comparing them requires time and information that most people lack, so the decision produces uncertainty rather than control. Digital platforms intensify this problem by offering endless options while suggesting that a better alternative remains available. Users may keep considering what they rejected and feel less satisfied with a suitable choice. The same pressure extends to major life decisions. Exposure to numerous career and lifestyle pathways can make commitment appear risky, leading people to delay action in search of an ideal option that may not exist. Clear categories, reliable reviews and sensible defaults would preserve freedom while reducing this avoidable mental load.\n\nIn conclusion, choice is valuable when it reflects substantial differences and allows people to meet individual needs. However, the modern excess of weakly differentiated options often wastes attention, complicates commitment and undermines contentment; in this sense, people frequently do have too many choices.",
