    id: "w-task2-c-42-small-family-units",
    title: "Small Family Units",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "family structure",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>In many countries today, people in cities either live alone or in small family units, rather than in large, extended family groups. Is this a positive or negative trend?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Urban residents increasingly live alone or with only their immediate family instead of sharing a home with several generations. Although smaller households offer privacy and independence, I believe this is a negative trend because it weakens everyday support networks and places greater pressure on both individuals and public services.\n\nThe main advantage of a small household is autonomy. Adults can organise their finances, routines and living space without negotiating every decision with relatives. This can reduce conflict between generations when views differ over careers, relationships or childcare. Couples may also develop a more equal partnership when household responsibilities are decided between them rather than shaped by established family roles. For young professionals who move to cities for work, living alone can represent confidence and independence rather than social isolation.\n\nNevertheless, the loss of extended-family living removes support that is difficult to replace. In a larger household, grandparents may supervise children while working parents contribute financially and assist older relatives. These exchanges distribute care across several adults and enable quick responses to illness or unemployment. When people live separately, the same needs require paid childcare, private nursing or overstretched public programmes, creating substantial costs. Emotional support also becomes less immediate. An older person living alone may have digital contact with relatives yet still lack companionship during meals or help in an emergency. Meanwhile, parents in small units can experience exhaustion because every domestic and caring responsibility falls on one or two people. Urban housing should permit privacy, but nearby homes and multigenerational developments could preserve independence without completely separating relatives.\n\nIn conclusion, smaller households enable greater freedom and may prevent some intergenerational tensions. On balance, however, the decline of extended-family living is negative because it reduces practical and emotional support while transferring care responsibilities to isolated individuals and costly formal services.",
