    id: "w-task2-c-43-energetic-engineering",
    title: "Genetic Engineering",
    task: "task2",
    type: "discussion",
    topic: "genetic engineering",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Genetic engineering is an important issue in society today. Some people think that it will improve people’s lives in many ways. Others feel that it may be a threat to life on earth. Discuss both these views and give your own opinion.</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Genetic engineering can alter medicine, agriculture and natural populations, prompting optimism and serious concern. Although poorly controlled applications could cause irreversible harm, I believe this technology can improve human life if its use is limited by rigorous testing and international regulation.\n\nOn the one hand, fears about genetic engineering are justified because biological changes may spread beyond their intended setting. A modified plant transferring engineered traits to wild relatives could disrupt ecosystems by making one species dominant. There are also ethical risks involving humans. If embryo editing moved from preventing disease to selecting appearance or intelligence, wealthy families might gain advantages that deepen inequality. Furthermore, genetic effects can emerge across generations, meaning that an apparently successful procedure may create problems that cannot easily be reversed. These possibilities make unrestricted experimentation a genuine threat rather than simple resistance to scientific progress.\n\nOn the other hand, governed genetic engineering offers substantial benefits. In medicine, correcting a defective gene could address the cause of an inherited disorder instead of requiring lifelong symptom management. In agriculture, disease-resistant or drought-tolerant crops can protect harvests as climate conditions become less predictable. This may reduce food insecurity and reliance on chemical pesticides, which harm soil and water. The crucial distinction is between responsible applications and avoidable enhancement or environmental release. Clinical treatments should pass independent safety reviews, while modified organisms require long-term monitoring and containment plans. International rules are necessary because genes and ecosystems do not respect national borders. With these safeguards, society can pursue high-value uses without accepting every technically possible experiment.\n\nIn conclusion, genetic engineering may threaten ecosystems and social equality when changes are released carelessly or used for human enhancement. Nevertheless, its capacity to treat serious disease and strengthen food production makes controlled development preferable to rejection, provided oversight remains strict and transparent.",
