    id: "w-task2-c-44-music",
    title: "Music",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "music",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Some people say that music is a good way of bringing people of different cultures and ages together. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this opinion?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Music is often described as a force that connects people across cultural and generational boundaries. I agree because shared musical experiences create immediate emotional contact, while the exchange of songs allows people to understand traditions and memories that may otherwise remain unfamiliar.\n\nOne reason music brings cultures together is that listeners can respond to rhythm, melody and performance even when they do not understand the lyrics. This gives strangers an accessible shared experience before they possess detailed knowledge of one another's language or customs. Festivals and online platforms then extend that initial interest by exposing audiences to instruments, stories and performance styles from other societies. For example, an international audience may first enjoy a Vietnamese folk-inspired song for its melody and later learn about the regional tradition behind it. Music does not remove cultural differences, but it can replace indifference with curiosity, making respectful conversation more likely.\n\nMusic also provides common ground between age groups. Older people attach songs to important periods of their lives, while younger listeners bring new interpretations to the same material. When families sing, attend performances or exchange playlists, these differences become resources for conversation rather than causes of distance. A grandparent explaining why a wartime song mattered can communicate personal history more vividly than a list of dates, and a modern cover may make it approachable to a teenager. Admittedly, musical taste can divide people when one generation dismisses another's preferences. Yet even disagreement encourages interaction, and community choirs require participants of different ages to listen and contribute towards a shared result.\n\nIn conclusion, music cannot by itself eliminate cultural prejudice or generational conflict. Nevertheless, its emotional accessibility and capacity to carry personal and collective stories make it an effective starting point for curiosity, conversation and cooperation among people who might otherwise have little contact.",
