    id: "w-task2-c-45-dress-code",
    title: "Dress Code",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "work",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Some people think that employers should not care about the way their employees dress, because what matters is the quality of their work. To what extent do you agree or disagree?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "The value of an employee is primarily determined by the standard of work they produce, rather than their appearance. Although strict dress codes are unnecessary in many occupations, I believe employers may reasonably require suitable clothing when it affects safety, hygiene, or public confidence.\n\nIn roles with little face-to-face contact, judging staff by their clothes can be both irrelevant and counterproductive. Software developers, graphic designers, and researchers, for example, are employed for their technical ability and the reliability of their output. Allowing them to dress comfortably does not reduce these qualities; it may instead improve concentration and signal that management trusts them to behave professionally. Moreover, rigid standards can disadvantage capable workers who cannot afford an extensive formal wardrobe or whose cultural clothing falls outside a narrow corporate norm. If performance is measured through clear targets and the quality of completed tasks, employers can make fairer decisions and create a more inclusive workplace.\n\nNevertheless, clothing is not entirely a private matter in every working environment. In hospitals, kitchens, and construction sites, uniforms and protective equipment reduce contamination or physical injury, so compliance directly influences the quality and safety of the service. Appearance can also form part of an employee’s professional responsibility when clients need immediate reassurance. A lawyer meeting a family during a serious legal dispute, for instance, may weaken the firm’s credibility by arriving in beachwear, even if their legal knowledge is excellent. Rather, employers should set broad, job-related expectations and explain their purpose, while giving staff freedom over colour, design, and culturally appropriate variations.\n\nIn conclusion, I largely agree that work quality should carry far more weight than appearance. However, employers are justified in setting proportionate dress requirements where clothing has a clear connection to safety, hygiene, or the trust of customers.",
