    id: "w-task2-c-46-parenting-course",
    title: "Parenting Course",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "family",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Caring for children is probably the most important job in any society. Because of this, all mothers and fathers should be required to take a course that prepares them to be good parents. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this view?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Raising children has far-reaching consequences for both families and society, so prospective parents would undoubtedly benefit from reliable guidance. However, I disagree that every mother and father should be legally required to complete a parenting course, because voluntary and accessible support would be fairer and more effective.\n\nThere is a strong case for making practical parenting education widely available. New parents must make decisions about nutrition, sleep, discipline, and emotional development, yet many rely on conflicting advice from relatives or social media. A well-designed course could explain basic child safety, non-violent discipline, and warning signs of illness, thereby preventing avoidable harm. It could also challenge outdated practices without blaming families. For example, community health centres could offer short sessions in the evenings or online, allowing parents to learn from paediatric nurses while discussing difficulties with others in the same situation.\n\nNevertheless, compulsion would create substantial practical and ethical problems. Parents have different cultural backgrounds, working hours, and levels of experience, and a standard course cannot define one universally correct way to raise a child. A legal requirement would also be difficult to enforce: governments would need to decide whether to fine exhausted new parents, delay access to services, or monitor attendance during pregnancy. These measures could punish low-income families most severely, even though they may face the greatest barriers to participation. Resources would be better directed towards free antenatal education, home visits for vulnerable households, and confidential advice after birth. When support is convenient and respectful, parents are more likely to seek help honestly and apply what they learn.\n\nIn conclusion, parenting education can improve children’s welfare and should be offered to every family. However, making a course compulsory would be inflexible and potentially punitive; removing barriers to voluntary, continuing support is a more constructive approach.",
