    id: "w-task2-c-47-literature-and-history",
    title: "Literature And History",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "education",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Some people believe that it is more important to teach children the literature and history of their own country, rather than the literature and history of other countries. To what extent do you agree or disagree?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Schools must decide how to divide limited classroom time between national and international literature and history. I agree that children should gain a firm understanding of their own country first, but this foundation should be complemented by substantial study of other societies.\n\nNational material is particularly important because it helps children understand the institutions, customs, and debates that shape their daily lives. Historical knowledge explains why national borders, public holidays, and systems of government exist, while literature gives pupils access to the language and experiences through which earlier generations understood their society. In Vietnam, for example, studying twentieth-century history can help young people make sense of memorial sites and family accounts that they encounter outside school. Reading Vietnamese stories from different regions can reveal that national culture is diverse rather than uniform. Without this shared frame of reference, students may know isolated global facts but remain unable to participate thoughtfully in civic life at home.\n\nHowever, giving national content near-exclusive priority would produce a narrow and potentially distorted education. Countries have never developed in isolation: migration, trade, war, and intellectual exchange connect their histories, so international material often makes domestic events clearer. Comparing several accounts of colonialism, for instance, teaches students that historical narratives depend partly on perspective and evidence. Foreign literature offers a similar benefit by placing readers inside unfamiliar lives, which can reduce simplistic assumptions about other cultures. A balanced curriculum might therefore devote more time in the early years to local foundations, before introducing progressively broader comparisons as pupils develop the skills to evaluate them.\n\nIn conclusion, a country’s own literature and history deserve a central place in its schools because they provide cultural and civic grounding. Nevertheless, children also need sustained international study to understand interdependence, question partial narratives, and engage responsibly with the wider world.",
