    id: "w-task2-c-48-power-of-advertising",
    title: "Power Of Advertising",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "media",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Today, the high sales of popular consumer goods reflect the power of advertising and not the real needs of the society in which they are sold. To what extent do you agree or disagree?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Popular consumer products are often supported by sophisticated campaigns that influence what people notice and desire. I largely agree that advertising, rather than social need, explains much of their sales, although marketing cannot sustain demand for products that offer no practical value.\n\nAdvertising powerfully shapes consumption by turning ordinary goods into symbols of identity and status. Companies repeatedly associate a brand with attractiveness, success, or belonging, so buyers begin to value the meaning attached to the product as much as its function. Digital advertising strengthens this effect because platforms can target individuals according to their searches, location, and previous purchases. A person whose phone works perfectly may therefore see promotions for a newer model, accompanied by reviews and limited-time offers that create a sense of urgency. When millions respond to these pressures, high sales indicate manufactured desire rather than an unmet social need. The frequent replacement of usable fashion and electronic products is evidence of this mechanism.\n\nThat said, advertising is not capable of creating unlimited demand from nothing. Consumers are more likely to repurchase goods that solve a real problem, provide convenience, or offer reliable quality. Affordable smartphones, for example, sell partly because they allow access to banking, education, navigation, and communication through one device. Furthermore, misleading promotion may produce an initial surge in purchases, but negative reviews and poor customer experiences can quickly damage a brand. Advertising is therefore best understood as an amplifier: it directs attention towards selected products and encourages people to buy sooner or more often, but the strongest products retain at least some connection to genuine preferences and needs.\n\nIn conclusion, the scale and speed of modern consumer sales often reveal how effectively advertising creates aspiration and accelerates replacement. Nevertheless, practical usefulness and product quality still place limits on what marketing alone can achieve.",
