    topic: "technology",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Some people believe that developments in the field of artificial intelligence will have a positive impact on our lives in the near future. Others, by contrast, are worried that we are not prepared for a world in which computers are more intelligent than humans. Discuss both of these views and give your own opinion.</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Artificial intelligence is expected to transform work and public services, yet its increasing capability has also raised concerns about human control. While AI can deliver substantial benefits in the near future, I believe society is insufficiently prepared for its economic and political consequences.\n\nOn the one hand, optimism is justified by AI’s ability to process information rapidly and perform routine tasks consistently. In healthcare, systems can assist doctors by identifying patterns in medical images, allowing serious conditions to be investigated earlier. Similar tools can translate educational materials, help disabled users communicate, and reduce the administrative burden on public employees. Businesses may also become more productive when repetitive documentation and basic customer enquiries are automated. Importantly, these systems do not need to exceed human intelligence in every respect to be useful; even specialised applications can save time and make valuable expertise more widely available.\n\nOn the other hand, technical progress is moving faster than the institutions intended to govern it. Employers can introduce automation immediately, whereas retraining workers and creating new forms of income protection may take years. AI can also reproduce bias hidden in training data, while highly convincing fabricated images and voices make fraud and political manipulation easier. More fundamentally, a small number of companies currently control the computing infrastructure and data needed to build the most capable models. If their systems become more influential than human decision-makers in recruitment, finance, or public debate, citizens may struggle to understand or challenge important outcomes. These risks do not require a fully conscious machine; they arise when powerful systems are deployed without accountability.\n\nIn conclusion, AI promises genuine improvements in medicine, accessibility, and productivity. Nevertheless, I share the concern that present safeguards are inadequate, and its benefits will depend on transparent regulation, worker support, and meaningful human oversight.",
