    id: "w-task2-c-50-cctv",
    title: "CCTV",
    task: "task2",
    type: "advantages-disadvantages",
    topic: "crime",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>In many cities the use of video cameras in public places is being increased in order to reduce crime, but some people believe that these measures restrict our individual freedom. Do the benefits of increased security outweigh the drawbacks?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Many urban authorities are expanding camera surveillance in streets, transport hubs, and other shared spaces to discourage crime. Although constant monitoring can threaten privacy and be misused, I believe its security benefits outweigh these drawbacks when access to recordings is strictly regulated.\n\nOn the one hand, widespread surveillance limits the ability to move through a city without leaving a detailed record. Footage combined with facial-recognition software can reveal where people travel, whom they meet, and which events they attend, even when they are suspected of no offence. This information could be exploited to monitor lawful protests, target minority groups, or track individuals for commercial purposes. Cameras installed for serious crime may later be used to punish minor behaviour. Such practices can make citizens feel watched rather than protected and may discourage legitimate participation in public life.\n\nOn the other hand, carefully governed cameras provide substantial practical advantages. Visible surveillance can deter opportunistic offences in places such as car parks and railway stations, where police cannot remain at every entrance. When crimes do occur, recordings help investigators reconstruct events, identify suspects, and test witness accounts against objective evidence. Cameras may also locate missing people or enable emergency services to respond quickly to dangerous crowding. Unlike intrusive searches, this protection operates without physically stopping every citizen. The strongest approach is therefore not to reject CCTV, but to require limited retention periods, independent authorisation for facial recognition, secure storage, and penalties for improper access. These safeguards preserve the evidential value of recordings while reducing the chance that surveillance becomes a general system of social control.\n\nIn conclusion, CCTV does impose a real cost on privacy. Nevertheless, its capacity to deter crime and support accurate investigations is more significant, provided that cities establish transparent rules that restrict how footage is collected, retained, and used.",
