    id: "w-task2-c-51-recycling",
    title: "Recycling",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "environment",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Some people claim that not enough of the waste from homes is recycled. They say that the only way to increase recycling is for governments to make it a legal requirement. To what extent do you think laws are needed to make people recycle more of their waste?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Household recycling rates remain disappointing, leading some people to call for a legal obligation to sort waste. I believe clear laws are necessary to establish minimum standards, but legislation will only succeed when governments also provide convenient services and public guidance.\n\nLegal requirements can change behaviour by making recycling a normal civic duty rather than an optional gesture. If households must separate paper, glass, food, and general rubbish, most residents will gradually incorporate the process into their daily routines. Rules also allow authorities to address persistent non-compliance through warnings and, as a last resort, modest fines. This is fairer than relying entirely on environmentally conscious individuals while others place avoidable costs on the community. Similar obligations can be imposed on landlords and housing managers, ensuring that apartment residents receive suitable bins and collection facilities instead of being blamed for a problem they cannot solve alone.\n\nHowever, punishment cannot compensate for a poorly designed recycling system. People are unlikely to comply if collection points are distant, instructions differ between districts, or recyclable material is collected too infrequently. Governments must therefore use legislation to organise producers and service providers as well as households. Standard labels on packaging, free colour-coded bins, regular collections, and deposit-return schemes would make the correct choice simple. Public information is equally important because contamination by food or unsuitable materials can cause an entire load to be rejected. Seoul’s approach to separating food waste illustrates the value of combining firm rules with dedicated containers and reliable collection. In such circumstances, enforcement supports an accessible system rather than merely penalising confusion.\n\nIn conclusion, laws are needed to set consistent expectations and prevent repeated refusal to recycle. Nevertheless, they are not the only solution: lasting improvement depends on convenient infrastructure, clear information, and shared responsibility across households, producers, and local authorities.",
