    id: "w-task2-c-52-extreme-sports",
    title: "Extreme Sports",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "sport",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Extreme sports such as sky diving and skiing are very dangerous and should be banned. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this view?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Activities such as skydiving and skiing expose participants to risks that are absent from most conventional sports. Nevertheless, I strongly disagree that they should be prohibited, because informed adults should retain personal choice and effective regulation can reduce avoidable danger.\n\nA blanket ban would be a disproportionate response to voluntary risk. Adults routinely make decisions that may harm them, from riding motorcycles to climbing mountains, and governments generally intervene only when these choices create an unacceptable threat to others. Extreme sports can also provide meaningful benefits: they demand discipline and physical preparation. In addition, mountain resorts and specialist centres support instructors, equipment suppliers, and local accommodation. Prohibiting these activities would remove legitimate employment and drive determined participants towards unlicensed locations, where equipment and emergency support are likely to be less reliable. The policy could therefore increase danger rather than eliminate it.\n\nThis does not mean that participants or operators should be free from responsibility. Governments can require instructors to hold recognised qualifications, oblige companies to inspect equipment, and suspend activities during unsafe weather. Beginners should receive clear information about the possibility of serious injury and complete supervised training before participating independently. Rules should also reflect differences between sports: controlled skiing on a marked slope does not present the same level of risk as jumping from an aircraft. New Zealand, for example, permits adventure tourism while imposing safety duties on commercial operators, demonstrating that access and protection are compatible. Where an activity creates direct risks for spectators or rescuers, authorities can restrict particular sites or conditions without banning the sport as a whole.\n\nIn conclusion, extreme sports can cause severe harm, but danger alone is not a sufficient reason for prohibition. Licensing, training, equipment standards, and targeted restrictions offer a more balanced way to protect the public while preserving individual freedom.",
