    id: "w-task2-car-ownership-traffic",
    title: "Car Ownership Traffic",
    task: "task2",
    type: "two-part",
    topic: "society",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Car ownership has increased so rapidly over the past thirty years that many cities in the world are now ‘one big traffic jam’. How true do you think this statement is? What measures can governments take to discourage people from using their cars?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Rapid growth in car ownership has made severe congestion a daily feature of cities, although the claim that they are one continuous traffic jam is an exaggeration outside peak hours. Governments can reduce car use by making driving less convenient while providing reliable alternatives.\n\nThe statement is largely accurate in dense urban centres because road capacity cannot expand as quickly as private vehicle numbers. During rush hours, thousands of commuters enter the same limited network, where one collision or badly parked vehicle can delay traffic across several districts. The problem is visible in cities such as Ho Chi Minh City, where cars occupy far more road space per passenger than motorcycles. Nevertheless, outer districts and roads at quieter times often remain relatively clear. Congestion is therefore widespread and economically damaging, but it varies by location and time rather than covering an entire city without interruption.\n\nEffective policies should address both the cost of driving and the reason people depend on cars. First, governments can introduce congestion charges in crowded central areas and raise parking fees. Once motorists must pay the full cost of occupying scarce road space, unnecessary trips become less attractive, while the revenue can fund transport improvements. However, restrictions will appear unfair unless commuters have a practical substitute. Cities should therefore develop frequent bus and rail services, create safe routes to stations and integrate fares across the network. Together, these policies reduce both the number of cars and the concentration of journeys that produces gridlock.\n\nIn conclusion, the description is broadly true during peak periods in many city centres, though not at all times or in every district. Pricing scarce road space and investing the proceeds in convenient public transport would discourage private car use most effectively.",
