    id: "w-task2-change-vs-routine",
    title: "Change Vs Routine",
    task: "task2",
    type: "discussion",
    topic: "society",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Some people prefer to spend their lives doing the same things and avoiding change. Others, however, think that change is always a good thing. Discuss both these views and give your own opinion.</p>",
    modelAnswer: "People differ over whether a stable routine or regular change leads to a better life. Although continuity can provide security and allow people to deepen their skills, I believe change is often necessary for growth and adaptation, but is not automatically beneficial in every circumstance.\n\nOn the one hand, avoiding unnecessary change can be a sensible choice. Familiar routines reduce uncertainty, which helps people plan their finances, maintain close relationships, and concentrate on long-term goals. Someone who remains in a suitable profession, for example, can build specialist knowledge over many years instead of repeatedly starting again as a beginner. Stability is particularly valuable when a change offers novelty rather than a genuine improvement. In such cases, preserving a successful arrangement is not a sign of fear, but a rational way to protect what already works.\n\nOn the other hand, excessive attachment to routine can prevent people from responding to new needs and opportunities. Economic conditions, technology, and personal responsibilities do not remain fixed; if individuals refuse to adjust, their skills may become outdated or their circumstances increasingly unsuitable. A worker who learns new digital tools, for instance, may become more productive and gain access to better roles. Change can also expose people to unfamiliar ideas, which encourages them to reassess weak habits and make more informed choices. Nevertheless, change should be judged by its likely consequences. Leaving a secure job without preparation or abandoning a supportive community merely for excitement may create avoidable harm rather than progress.\n\nIn conclusion, routine offers security, expertise, and continuity, whereas well-considered change enables people to adapt and develop. I therefore regard change as frequently necessary and beneficial, but reject the claim that it is always good.",
