    task: "task2",
    type: "two-part",
    topic: "education",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Art is considered an important part of a society as well as an expression of its culture. Do you think it is important for children to be taught art? Do you think children should be encouraged to focus on art rather than other subjects?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Art records a society’s values and gives people a means of expressing ideas that words cannot always capture. I therefore believe that every child should receive art education, but it should complement rather than replace sustained study of other subjects.\n\nTeaching art is important because it develops both practical abilities and cultural understanding. When pupils draw, paint or model objects, they must observe details, make choices and revise work that does not produce the intended effect. This process strengthens concentration and creative problem-solving, skills that also support learning elsewhere. Art lessons can additionally introduce children to the images, crafts and architecture through which communities preserve their history. Vietnamese pupils who study Đông Hồ folk paintings, for example, can learn how earlier generations represented family life and local customs. Without guided exposure at school, many children, particularly those whose families rarely visit galleries, may never encounter this part of their cultural inheritance.\n\nHowever, encouraging most children to prioritise art over other subjects would create an unnecessary imbalance. Literacy, mathematics and science provide foundations that young people need for everyday decisions and a wide range of careers. Since children’s interests and abilities are still developing, specialising too early could also close off future options before they understand the consequences. Schools should instead guarantee regular art lessons while maintaining a broad curriculum, then offer additional studio time to pupils who demonstrate strong interest or talent. In this way, a prospective designer can develop a portfolio without another child being pressured to sacrifice subjects more closely aligned with their goals.\n\nIn conclusion, art deserves a secure place in every child’s education because it builds creativity and connects pupils with their culture. Nevertheless, it should normally remain one element of a balanced programme, with greater specialisation reserved for those who actively choose it.",
