    topic: "society",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>In many parts of the world, children and teenagers are spending more and more of their time indoors. What do you think is the cause of this problem? What measures could best be taken to solve it?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Young people in many countries spend an increasing share of their lives indoors. This trend mainly reflects the attraction of screen-based entertainment and a shortage of safe outdoor spaces, so effective solutions must make digital use less dominant and neighbourhood activities more accessible.\n\nOne central cause is that indoor entertainment offers immediate rewards with almost no effort. Games, streaming services, and social platforms provide constantly changing content, while personalised recommendations encourage children to remain online. Outdoor play, by comparison, requires companions, suitable weather, and somewhere safe to go. This disadvantage is particularly serious in densely populated cities, where heavy traffic and limited public space make parents reluctant to let children leave home independently. Academic pressure further reinforces the pattern. After a long school day, many pupils attend extra classes or complete substantial homework, leaving little daylight or energy for exercise.\n\nMeasures should address each of these causes directly. Parents and schools can establish consistent limits on recreational screen time while offering attractive alternatives, such as team sports, gardening clubs, and weekend nature trips. Limits are more likely to work when young people gain a rewarding substitute rather than merely losing access to a device. At the same time, local authorities should convert vacant land into parks, improve lighting, and create protected walking and cycling routes. These changes would reduce parental safety concerns and allow children to meet friends without travelling far. Finally, schools could coordinate homework and extracurricular demands so that several evenings remain free. A city neighbourhood with a nearby court and traffic-free paths, for example, gives teenagers a reason and opportunity to spend time outside.\n\nIn conclusion, persuasive digital entertainment, unsafe urban environments, and crowded academic timetables keep young people indoors. Screen boundaries, appealing organised activities, safer spaces, and more balanced schedules would therefore provide an effective response.",
