    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>In many countries, children are engaged in some kind of paid work. Some people regard this as completely wrong, while others consider it as valuable work experience, important for learning and taking responsibility. What are your opinions on this issue?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "The involvement of children in paid employment is often viewed either as exploitation or as useful preparation for adult life. In my view, its value depends on the child’s age and working conditions: light, age-appropriate work can be beneficial, whereas labour that threatens education, health or safety is unacceptable.\n\nSuitable employment can teach young people lessons that are difficult to acquire in a classroom. When children earn a modest amount through tasks such as delivering newspapers or helping in a family shop, they learn that income requires time and effort. This understanding can make them more careful with money and more reliable when completing assigned duties. Employment may also develop communication skills because young workers must follow instructions and deal politely with customers or colleagues. Provided that the hours are limited, these experiences can strengthen responsibility without interfering with schoolwork.\n\nHowever, paid work becomes harmful when children are too young or employers place commercial interests above their welfare. Long shifts leave pupils physically tired and reduce the time available for homework, which can gradually weaken their academic performance and future prospects. More seriously, work in factories, construction sites or other hazardous environments exposes children to injuries that they are neither physically nor emotionally prepared to handle. Because young workers have limited knowledge of their rights, they are also particularly vulnerable to low wages and intimidation. Governments should therefore enforce minimum-age and safety regulations while allowing carefully supervised forms of part-time work.\n\nIn conclusion, paid employment is not inherently wrong for children. It can provide valuable experience when it is safe, limited and appropriate to their maturity, but any work that exploits them or disrupts their education should be prohibited.",
