    id: "w-task2-colour-schemes-mood",
    title: "Colour Schemes Mood",
    task: "task2",
    type: "two-part",
    topic: "health",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Psychologists have known for many years that colour can affect how people feel. For this reason, attention should be given to colour schemes when decorating places such as offices and hospitals. How true is this statement? How far does colour influence people&#x27;s health and capacity for work?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Colour can shape people's immediate emotional response to an interior, so it deserves consideration in workplaces and healthcare facilities. However, I believe its influence on health and productivity is real but limited, because practical conditions matter more.\n\nThoughtful colour choices can make institutional spaces feel calmer, brighter, or more welcoming. Muted natural shades may reduce the harshness of a hospital ward, while clear contrasts between walls, doors, and signs can help patients navigate an unfamiliar building. In an office, avoiding extremely dark or visually overwhelming schemes may reduce distraction and create a more comfortable atmosphere. Colour can also support the intended purpose of different areas: a quiet consultation room may benefit from subdued tones, whereas a shared creative space can use stronger accents to provide visual energy. Designers should therefore treat colour as a functional element rather than a purely decorative choice.\n\nNevertheless, colour alone has little power to determine whether people remain healthy or work effectively. In hospitals, recovery depends primarily on appropriate treatment, hygiene, rest, and attentive staff. A soothing blue wall cannot compensate for constant noise, overcrowding, or delayed care. Likewise, office productivity is shaped more directly by manageable workloads, supportive supervision, suitable equipment, ventilation, and natural light. An employee facing unrealistic deadlines will not perform substantially better because a room has been painted green. Responses to colour are also subjective and culturally influenced, so one scheme may relax some occupants while appearing cold to others. Its main value is therefore indirect: colour can complement a well-designed environment, but it cannot correct fundamental failures in healthcare or working conditions.\n\nIn conclusion, attention to colour schemes is justified because colour affects atmosphere, comfort, and navigation. Its contribution to health and work capacity, however, remains secondary to medical care, organisational practice, and the physical quality of the space.",
