    id: "w-task2-doctors-vs-healthy-lifestyle",
    title: "Doctors Vs Healthy Lifestyle",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "health",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>These days, too many people maintain their health by relying on doctors and medicine, rather than by following a healthy lifestyle. To what extent do you agree with this statement?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Modern medicine enables many people to manage conditions that would previously have shortened their lives. Nevertheless, I largely agree that excessive reliance on medical treatment has displaced healthier daily habits, especially in relation to preventable chronic disease.\n\nDoctors and medicines are indispensable, and using them should not automatically be considered unhealthy dependence. Infections may require antibiotics, while people with asthma, diabetes or heart disease often need continuing treatment even when they exercise and eat carefully. Regular screening can also identify serious illness before symptoms become obvious, greatly improving the chance of recovery. Advising such patients to replace professional care with lifestyle changes would be dangerous. Furthermore, some health problems are influenced by age, genetics or environmental exposure, so personal discipline cannot prevent every disease. Medical care should therefore remain a central part of public health rather than a last resort.\n\nHowever, treatment is increasingly used to manage consequences that healthier routines could reduce. Sedentary work, convenient processed food and car-dependent travel make excessive calorie intake and insufficient exercise normal parts of daily life. When medication lowers blood pressure or controls blood sugar, patients may feel less urgency to address these underlying behaviours. The illness is then managed rather than prevented, creating continuing costs and leaving the individual vulnerable to related conditions. For example, tablets may control one indicator of cardiovascular risk, but they cannot fully reproduce the benefits that regular movement provides for weight, sleep and mental well-being. Governments and doctors should make prevention easier by designing walkable neighbourhoods, regulating misleading food advertising and prescribing practical lifestyle support alongside medicine.\n\nIn conclusion, medical treatment remains essential for acute, inherited and unavoidable conditions. Yet too many preventable illnesses are addressed only after they develop, so healthcare systems should place greater emphasis on daily activity, nutritious diets and other forms of prevention.",
