    id: "w-task2-endangered-species-save",
    title: "Endangered Species Save",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "environment",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Many people feel it is a waste to try to save endangered animal species, for example the tiger or the blue whale. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this statement?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Some people consider protecting endangered animals, such as tigers and blue whales, an unjustified use of limited resources. I strongly disagree because conservation preserves ecological systems on which people depend and can address environmental damage far beyond a single animal.\n\nThe disappearance of a species can destabilise an entire food web. Large predators regulate the number and behaviour of prey animals; without this pressure, herbivore populations may grow excessively and consume vegetation faster than it can recover. The resulting habitat decline affects smaller animals, water retention, and even nearby agriculture. Marine species perform similarly important functions. Whales transport nutrients through the ocean and support microscopic organisms that absorb carbon. Protecting them therefore contributes to climate regulation rather than merely keeping an impressive animal alive. Since ecological relationships are complex and difficult to rebuild, preventing extinction is safer and cheaper than attempting to repair a collapsed ecosystem.\n\nConservation programmes can also protect extensive habitats and local livelihoods. Tigers need large connected territories, so safeguarding their range preserves forests that contain many less visible species as well as rivers used by surrounding communities. If these areas are managed responsibly, wildlife tourism can provide employment for guides, accommodation providers, and park staff, giving residents a financial reason to resist illegal hunting and land clearance. Admittedly, poorly planned projects may direct too much money towards a famous animal while neglecting urgent human needs. This is an argument for careful project selection, not abandonment. Governments and charities can prioritise programmes that protect habitats, involve local people, and publish measurable outcomes.\n\nIn conclusion, saving endangered animals is not wasteful when conservation targets the ecosystems around them. By maintaining food webs, preserving forests and oceans, and supporting sustainable local income, such efforts generate benefits that extend well beyond the survival of individual species.",
