    id: "w-task2-family-history-research",
    title: "Family History Research",
    task: "task2",
    type: "two-part",
    topic: "society",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>In some parts of the world it is becoming popular to research the history of one’s own family. Why might people want to do this? Is it a positive or negative development?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Researching one’s ancestry has become common in many societies. People are motivated by a desire for personal identity and stronger family connections, and I regard this trend as positive provided that historical findings are interpreted responsibly.\n\nOne motivation is that mobility has weakened people’s sense of origin. When families move between regions or countries, younger generations may grow up with limited knowledge of the communities and traditions that shaped their relatives. Examining records, photographs, and oral histories can fill this gap and give individuals a clearer account of where they belong. Family research can also bring relatives together. Interviewing older family members encourages rare conversations, while constructing a family tree requires cousins and siblings to share memories and documents. Some motives are practical: people investigate inherited medical conditions or seek records needed to claim citizenship through ancestry.\n\nOverall, this is a beneficial development because it preserves knowledge that could disappear. Personal accounts of migration, work, and family life add human detail to official history, and recording them allows future generations to understand how events affected ordinary people. The process can also increase empathy within families. Learning that an ancestor made difficult choices during war or economic hardship may help younger relatives judge previous generations less harshly. Nevertheless, genealogical research has risks. Commercial DNA services may expose sensitive information, and uncertain evidence can produce exaggerated claims about ethnicity or status. These problems arise from careless methods rather than the activity itself. With consent, secure handling of data, and honest acknowledgement of uncertainty, the advantages remain greater.\n\nIn conclusion, people explore family history to understand their identity, connect with relatives, and sometimes obtain practical information. Since this research preserves valuable memories and can deepen understanding across generations, it is largely a positive trend when conducted with care.",
