    id: "w-task2-farming-science-benefits",
    title: "Farming Science Benefits",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "technology",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Scientific developments in farming always bring major benefits. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this statement?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Scientific advances have transformed the way food is produced. Although many innovations raise yields and make farming more reliable, I disagree that they always deliver benefits, since poorly controlled technologies can damage ecosystems and exclude smaller producers.\n\nThere is no doubt that agricultural science has generated substantial gains. Improved seed varieties can resist disease or tolerate drought, allowing farmers to harvest more food from the same area of land. This greater productivity helps stabilise supplies and limits the need to clear forests for cultivation. Precision technologies also enable growers to apply water, fertiliser, and pesticides only where they are required. Sensors that identify unusually dry soil, for example, can direct irrigation to a specific section of a field instead of watering the entire farm. Such systems reduce waste, lower production costs, and make crops more resilient to increasingly unpredictable weather.\n\nHowever, an innovation is not automatically beneficial merely because it is scientifically advanced. Intensive dependence on chemical fertilisers may increase output initially, yet repeated overuse can degrade soil and contaminate nearby rivers. Similarly, a pest-resistant crop may encourage large areas of genetic uniformity, leaving the food system vulnerable if a new disease emerges. The distribution of benefits is also uneven. Automated machinery and patented seeds often require significant investment, so large agricultural companies can adopt them while smallholders incur debt or lose access to markets. In some regions, replacing seasonal labour with harvesting machines may improve efficiency but remove an important source of rural income. These outcomes result not from science alone but from introducing it without adequate testing, regulation, or support for affected communities.\n\nIn conclusion, farming science frequently improves productivity, resilience, and the careful use of resources. Nevertheless, because it can also create environmental harm and deepen economic inequality, its benefits are neither universal nor guaranteed.",
