    id: "w-task2-fast-food-tax",
    title: "Fast Food Tax",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "health",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>In some countries an increasing number of people are suffering from health problems as a result of eating too much fast food. It is therefore necessary for governments to impose a higher tax on this kind of food. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this opinion?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Excessive consumption of fast food is contributing to diet-related illness. I largely agree that these products should be taxed more heavily, although taxation must be combined with measures that make healthier food genuinely accessible.\n\nA higher tax can change both consumer and business behaviour. Fast food is attractive partly because meals high in salt, sugar and fat are cheap and immediately available. If taxation raises their price, frequent customers have a financial reason to reduce impulse purchases or choose smaller portions. At the same time, restaurant chains may reformulate meals to avoid the highest tax rate, so the policy can improve what people eat. Governments could then direct the revenue towards nutrition education and treatment for conditions associated with poor diets. Taxes on sugary drinks illustrate this mechanism: their purpose is not to ban a product, but to ensure that its price reflects some of the health costs created by excessive consumption.\n\nHowever, tax alone would be both limited and potentially unfair. Low-income workers often rely on fast food because long working hours, limited cooking facilities or the absence of affordable shops leave them with few convenient alternatives. Raising prices without changing these conditions would reduce household budgets rather than improve diets. Governments should therefore use part of the revenue to subsidise fruit, vegetables and healthy school meals, while requiring large chains to display clear nutritional information. Such policies expand practical choice; once healthier meals are affordable, visible and convenient, the price signal created by the tax is far more likely to alter daily habits instead of simply punishing consumers.\n\nIn conclusion, I support a higher tax on unhealthy fast food because it can discourage overconsumption and encourage reformulation. Nevertheless, it is necessary only as part of a broader policy that makes nutritious alternatives easier for all groups to obtain.",
