    id: "w-task2-fatherhood-motherhood",
    title: "Fatherhood Motherhood",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "society",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Fatherhood ought to be emphasized as much as motherhood. The idea that women are solely responsible for deciding whether or not to have babies leads on to the idea that they are also responsible for bringing the children up. To what extent do you agree or disagree?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Women have traditionally been treated as the main decision-makers and caregivers in matters concerning children. I strongly agree that fatherhood should receive equal emphasis because family planning and child-rearing are shared responsibilities, even though pregnancy directly affects the mother’s body.\n\nAdmittedly, mothers have a distinctive biological role. They experience pregnancy and childbirth, and many provide most early feeding and physical care. Consequently, the woman’s health and consent must remain central to any decision about having a baby. However, this does not mean that she should make family-planning decisions without meaningful participation from her partner. Having a child affects household finances, employment and long-term commitments for both adults. When fathers regard the decision as belonging exclusively to women, they can more easily distance themselves from the obligations that follow.\n\nEqual emphasis on fatherhood would encourage men to become active parents rather than occasional helpers. If both parents discuss parenthood beforehand, they are more likely to divide childcare, household work and financial duties deliberately. This reduces the risk that mothers will leave employment simply because care is assumed to be their natural role. Children also benefit because consistent involvement from two parents provides broader emotional support and supervision. Parental-leave schemes that reserve time specifically for fathers, for example, signal that caring for an infant is a normal male responsibility. As workplaces and families adopt this expectation, fathers gain practical experience early, making continued participation more likely as the child grows.\n\nIn conclusion, women must retain control over medical decisions affecting their bodies, but the broader choice to form a family and the duty of raising children should involve both parents. Fatherhood therefore deserves as much social recognition as motherhood.",
