    id: "w-task2-food-waste-shops-restaurants",
    title: "Food Waste Shops Restaurants",
    task: "task2",
    type: "problem-solution",
    topic: "food",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>In some countries, people waste a lot of food which is bought in shops and restaurants. What do you think are the reasons? What can be done to solve this problem?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "In some countries, large amounts of food bought from shops and restaurants end up unused. This waste is driven mainly by overbuying and premature discarding of still-edible food, and it can be reduced through clearer guidance and better leftover practices.\n\nFood waste of this kind is largely rooted in everyday habits after purchase. Many households and diners routinely buy more than they need: supermarket promotions encourage bulk shopping, while restaurants serve oversized portions to signal value for money. Once food sits unused after a planned meal, or is left unfinished on a plate, discarding it feels easier than storing leftovers carefully, so convenience outweighs thrift. A second habit is throwing out food that still looks acceptable but no longer matches buyers’ expectations of freshness. Shoppers often take home items close to a labelled date and then bin them at the first soft texture or dull colour, even when the food remains safe; diners leave unfinished dishes for the same reason. This confusion about dates and appearance, rather than genuine spoilage, is what turns still-edible purchases into waste.\n\nThese habits can be reduced by targeting both behaviours directly. Clearer date labelling and campaigns explaining the difference between quality guidance and genuine safety limits would make people less likely to bin food simply because it looks imperfect. When shoppers realise that a soft tomato or a nearing “best before” date does not mean the product is unsafe, premature discarding falls. Overbuying can be tackled at the point of sale and service: shops can scale back aggressive bulk promotions, while restaurants offer smaller default portions, pay-by-weight options, and leftover packaging as a routine service. If buying less becomes the easy choice, and taking unfinished food home feels normal, far less purchased food ends up in the bin.\n\nIn conclusion, food waste from shops and restaurants stems mainly from overpurchasing and premature discarding of still-edible food. Clearer labelling and more flexible buying and portioning practices can curb the problem.",
