    type: "advantages-disadvantages",
    topic: "education",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Some experts believe that it is better for children to begin learning a foreign language at primary school rather than secondary school. Do the advantages of this outweigh the disadvantages?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Some educational experts argue that children should start a foreign language in primary school instead of waiting until secondary school. I believe the advantages of this earlier start outweigh the drawbacks.\n\nOn the one hand, early language programmes create real practical problems. Many primary teachers are general classroom teachers rather than language specialists, so the quality of lessons can vary widely from school to school. If specialist teachers have to be hired instead, schools may lose the timetable flexibility that makes short, frequent lessons possible. There is also a risk at the next stage: secondary schools may receive pupils with very different levels in different languages, which can slow whole-class progress and demotivate children who once enjoyed the subject. In that sense, a poorly planned early start can undo some of the gains it was meant to create.\n\nOn the other hand, the benefits are stronger when the policy is carried out carefully. Young children usually pick up pronunciation and basic patterns more easily, partly because they are less self-conscious than teenagers and more willing to practise through games and songs. Starting earlier also gives them more total hours of contact with the language before they leave school, which makes later study less of a sudden jump. Over time, this early exposure can support not only language skill but also a more open attitude to other cultures. These gains matter for both individual learning and a society that increasingly needs people who can communicate across languages.\n\nIn conclusion, although teacher supply and uneven levels at secondary school are genuine concerns, I believe the advantages of beginning a foreign language in primary school outweigh the disadvantages, provided schools plan the transition carefully.",
