    id: "w-task2-formal-education-start-age",
    title: "Formal Education Start Age",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "education",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>In some countries, it is thought advisable that children begin formal education at four years old, while in others they do not have to start school until they are seven or eight. How far do you agree with either of these views?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Countries differ in the age at which children enter formal education, with some beginning at four and others waiting until seven or eight. I favour the later age, although younger children should still have access to play-based learning.\n\nStarting school at four may appear beneficial because it gives children earlier instruction in literacy, numeracy, and classroom behaviour. This is valuable for those from disadvantaged homes, where books, educational activities, or adult support may be limited. A well-funded programme can narrow this gap by providing language-rich interaction and identifying developmental difficulties early. Attendance also enables parents to return to work, improving household income. However, these benefits do not require a formal academic environment. They can be delivered through preschool programmes that use stories, games, and guided exploration rather than tests, homework, and prolonged desk-based lessons.\n\nFormal schooling is more suitable from seven or eight because children are better prepared emotionally and cognitively for structured study. At four, attention spans and self-regulation are still developing; requiring sustained concentration can create frustration, which children may associate with learning itself. Delaying academic instruction allows them to build language, physical coordination, and social skills through play. These foundations improve readiness, so lessons can progress more efficiently and early gains made by younger starters may soon disappear. Finland, for instance, delays formal schooling while providing high-quality early childhood education, showing that a later start need not prevent strong academic outcomes. The essential distinction is therefore between delaying education and delaying formal instruction: children should keep learning, but through methods appropriate to their stage of development.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree more strongly with beginning formal education at seven or eight, as greater maturity supports effective and positive learning. Before then, play-based provision can deliver the social and developmental benefits of early education without imposing premature academic demands.",
