    id: "w-task2-gap-year-work-travel",
    title: "Gap Year Work Travel",
    task: "task2",
    type: "advantages-disadvantages",
    topic: "education",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>In some countries young people are encouraged to work or travel for a year between finishing high school and starting university studies. Discuss the advantages and disadvantages for young people who decide to do this.</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Taking a year to work or travel before university has become an established option for young people in some countries. Although this decision can delay academic progress and create financial difficulties, a well-planned gap year generally offers valuable practical experience and greater maturity.\n\nThe main disadvantages concern cost and the loss of study habits. International travel can require substantial spending on transport and accommodation, meaning that students from wealthier families have much greater access to the experience. Those who work may save money, but low-skilled temporary jobs do not always provide meaningful development. Furthermore, after a year without formal study, some young people may find it difficult to return to demanding reading, deadlines and examinations. If they become comfortable with a regular income, they may even decide not to enter university, despite the long-term benefits that a degree could provide.\n\nOn the other hand, purposeful work or travel can prepare school-leavers for university and adult life. Employment requires punctuality, cooperation and responsible money management, so participants often become more independent before living away from home. Travel can likewise expose them to unfamiliar customs and practical challenges, which encourages adaptability and a broader outlook. These experiences may also improve academic decisions. A student who works in a care home, for example, can develop a more realistic understanding of healthcare before committing to a nursing degree. Consequently, young people may begin university with clearer goals and stronger motivation, reducing the likelihood that they will abandon an unsuitable course.\n\nIn conclusion, a gap year carries genuine risks, particularly financial inequality and reduced academic momentum. Nevertheless, when it includes structured employment or purposeful travel, its contribution to maturity and informed study choices is likely to outweigh these drawbacks.",
