    type: "opinion",
    topic: "culture",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Countries are becoming more and more similar because people are able to buy the same products anywhere in the world. Do you think this is a positive or negative development?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "The global availability of identical consumer products is making countries increasingly alike. Although this trend offers greater choice and more consistent quality, I believe its overall effect is negative because it weakens local businesses and gradually reduces visible cultural diversity.\n\nAdmittedly, international products can improve consumers’ lives. Competition from global companies may encourage domestic producers to raise safety and quality standards, while mass production can make reliable goods more affordable. Travellers and migrants also benefit from finding familiar medicines, electronic devices or food brands abroad, especially during travel or emergencies. However, these gains mainly concern convenience, and access to the same goods does not require every shopping street to become commercially identical. The benefits can still be preserved if imported products exist alongside strong local alternatives.\n\nThe greater concern is that global brands often possess advertising budgets, distribution networks and purchasing power that small businesses cannot match. As consumers shift towards familiar international names, local shops lose revenue and eventually close. This process removes not only economic opportunities but also products that reflect regional materials, skills and tastes. For example, if a traditional market is replaced by chain stores selling standardised clothing and food, visitors and residents encounter fewer features that distinguish that place from any other city. Once local producers disappear, rebuilding their knowledge and supply networks is difficult. Countries may consequently become more dependent on a small number of multinational companies while sacrificing the everyday expressions of culture that give communities a distinct identity.\n\nIn conclusion, globally available products bring useful choice and dependable standards, but these advantages are outweighed by the loss of local enterprise and cultural character. International trade should broaden what consumers can buy rather than make commercial environments uniform.",
