    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Happiness is considered very important in life. Why is it difficult to define? What factors are important in achieving happiness?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Although happiness is widely regarded as a central aim of life, there is no definition that applies equally to everyone. This is because well-being is shaped by personal expectations and cultural values, while its achievement generally depends on security, supportive relationships and a sense of purpose.\n\nHappiness is difficult to define primarily because people evaluate their lives against different standards. An ambitious professional may associate it with achievement and recognition, whereas a retired person may value peaceful routines and time with family. These priorities can also change as individuals age or face new circumstances, so even one person’s definition is rarely permanent. Culture creates further variation. Societies that emphasise individual success may connect happiness with independence, while more family-centred communities often place greater importance on belonging and shared responsibilities. Happiness is therefore a subjective judgement rather than a fixed condition that can be measured through one universal indicator.\n\nNevertheless, several broad factors make lasting contentment more likely. Basic financial and physical security are essential because people who struggle to afford food, housing or medical care must focus on immediate survival. Once these needs are met, close relationships become increasingly important. Regular emotional support reduces isolation and helps individuals recover from setbacks instead of facing them alone. Finally, people need some control over their decisions and a worthwhile goal, whether this involves raising a family, developing a career or contributing to their community. Material comfort without meaningful activity may produce convenience, but it seldom provides sustained fulfilment.\n\nIn conclusion, happiness resists a single definition because expectations differ across people, cultures and stages of life. However, personal security, strong social bonds and purposeful activity form a reliable foundation for achieving it.",
