    id: "w-task2-high-taxes-public-services",
    title: "High Taxes Public Services",
    task: "task2",
    type: "discussion",
    topic: "society",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Some people feel that governments should take a large proportion of people’s salaries to pay for necessary public services such as roads and schools. Others feel that high taxes are a bad thing. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.</p>",
    modelAnswer: "People disagree over whether governments should collect a large share of personal income to fund essential services or keep taxation low. Although excessive rates can weaken incentives and strain household budgets, I believe high taxes are justified when revenue is managed transparently and delivers reliable public services.\n\nOn the one hand, opponents argue that high income taxes reduce personal freedom and economic activity. When workers surrender part of each pay rise, the reward for working longer hours or acquiring advanced skills becomes smaller. Some may therefore limit their effort, while qualified employees may move to lower-tax jurisdictions, reducing the domestic skills base. Heavy taxation also leaves families with less disposable income for housing, childcare, and savings. This is particularly unfair where public agencies waste revenue, because citizens pay more without receiving better services. In such circumstances, high rates can undermine trust rather than promote social welfare.\n\nOn the other hand, taxation allows governments to provide infrastructure and opportunities that individual spending cannot secure efficiently. A well-maintained road network lowers delays, enabling firms to move goods reliably and supporting productivity. Similarly, publicly funded schools give children from low-income families access to qualified teachers and learning materials. This improves their employment prospects and, over time, broadens the tax base that sustains future services. Universal provision also spreads risks: one household may never require expensive emergency treatment, but pooled revenue ensures that care is available when anyone needs it. Provided that spending is audited and clearly linked to public outcomes, the collective gains from strong services outweigh the reduction in private income.\n\nIn conclusion, high taxes can discourage effort and become indefensible when governments spend poorly. Nevertheless, I support comparatively high taxation because effective roads, schools, and other shared services increase opportunity and economic capacity in ways that private purchasing alone cannot achieve.",
