    task: "task2",
    type: "discussion",
    topic: "education",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Some people believe that school children should not be given homework by their teachers, whereas others argue that homework plays an important role in the education of children. Discuss both of these views and give your own opinion.</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Whether teachers should assign homework to schoolchildren remains widely debated. Although excessive work after school can harm children’s well-being, I believe that limited, purposeful assignments make a valuable contribution to their education.\n\nOn the one hand, there are sound reasons for removing homework altogether. Pupils already spend much of the day following a fixed timetable, and additional tasks can reduce the time available for rest, exercise and family interaction. This is especially concerning for younger children, whose concentration and emotional regulation depend partly on adequate sleep and unstructured play. Homework can also deepen inequality because not every pupil has a quiet room, internet access or an adult who can provide assistance. Consequently, a task intended to reinforce learning may instead reward home circumstances and cause struggling children to feel that they are falling further behind.\n\nOn the other hand, carefully designed homework remains educationally useful. Short exercises allow pupils to retrieve knowledge without immediate teacher support, revealing what they genuinely understand rather than what they can follow during a guided lesson. Regular reading, multiplication practice or revision before a test can therefore consolidate essential skills and help teachers identify gaps in the next class. Assignments also teach older pupils to plan their time and meet deadlines, which prepares them for independent study at university and for employment. These benefits do not justify nightly workloads of several hours; rather, schools should coordinate tasks across subjects and set work whose purpose is clear. A brief mathematics problem set, for instance, is more defensible than copying pages of notes merely to keep children occupied.\n\nIn conclusion, opponents of homework rightly highlight pressure and unequal home support. Nevertheless, I favour retaining it in a restrained form because focused practice strengthens learning and gradually develops independence.",
