    id: "w-task2-human-threat-wildlife",
    title: "Human Threat Wildlife",
    task: "task2",
    type: "problem-solution",
    topic: "society",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Recent research has confirmed that ‘human activity has become the greatest threat to plant and animal life’. Why do you think this has happened? How can we reduce our impact on the natural world?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Human activity now poses a danger to wildlife because expanding consumption has transformed habitats faster than many species can adapt. Reducing this pressure requires governments to change the economic incentives behind environmental damage and individuals to adopt less resource-intensive habits.\n\nThe main cause is the scale at which land and natural resources are being exploited. As populations and incomes grow, demand increases for food, housing, energy and consumer goods. Forests are consequently cleared for farms and roads, rivers are altered by dams, and oceans are intensively fished. These changes do not merely remove individual animals; they fragment breeding areas and disrupt food chains on which entire ecosystems depend. Pollution and climate change intensify the damage. Plastic waste can remain in marine environments for decades, while rising temperatures shift seasonal conditions too rapidly for some plants and animals to migrate or reproduce successfully. Modern production has therefore combined local habitat loss with environmental changes that operate across the planet.\n\nEffective action must address both industry and consumption. Governments can remove subsidies that encourage overfishing or forest clearance and require businesses to pay for restoring damaged land. Protected areas should also be connected by wildlife corridors, enabling species to move between habitats as conditions change. Costa Rica, for example, has expanded forest cover by rewarding landowners who preserve trees, demonstrating how financial policy can support conservation. At a personal level, consumers can reduce food waste, choose durable products and eat fewer resource-intensive foods. Better product labels and accessible public transport can make these choices easier rather than placing the entire burden on individual goodwill.\n\nIn conclusion, wildlife is threatened because human demand drives habitat destruction, pollution and climate change on an unprecedented scale. Strong regulation, economic incentives and more restrained consumption can reduce this impact while allowing societies to meet essential needs.",
