    id: "w-task2-internet-communication-vs-information",
    title: "Internet Communication Vs Information",
    task: "task2",
    type: "discussion",
    topic: "technology",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>It is generally believed that the Internet is an excellent means of communication, but some people suggest that it may not be the best place to find information. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.</p>",
    modelAnswer: "The Internet enables rapid communication, yet its reliability as an information source remains disputed. While online material varies in quality, I believe it is also an excellent research tool when users evaluate sources carefully.\n\nOn the one hand, digital communication is immediate, inexpensive, and largely independent of distance. Messages, documents, and video calls can reach large groups within seconds, allowing families, businesses, and public institutions to coordinate efficiently. A company with staff in several countries, for example, can discuss a project in real time without the cost and delay of travel. Online platforms support different formats, so users can choose text for an update or video for a detailed conversation. This combination of speed, reach, and flexibility explains why the Internet has become indispensable for communication.\n\nOn the other hand, finding trustworthy information online can be difficult because anyone can publish content. Search engines may rank material by popularity or relevance rather than accuracy, while social platforms reward dramatic claims. False information can therefore spread quickly, and repetition may make an unsupported claim appear credible. Nevertheless, these weaknesses do not make the Internet an inferior source by nature. It provides direct access to university research, government records, reputable journalism, and specialist databases that would otherwise be costly or geographically inaccessible. Effective research depends on checking authorship, publication dates, evidence, and agreement between independent sources. When users apply these tests, the enormous range of online material becomes an advantage because claims can be compared rather than accepted in isolation. The real limitation is thus inadequate information literacy, not the medium itself.\n\nIn conclusion, the Internet is effective for communication because it removes barriers of time, distance, and cost. Despite the presence of unreliable content, I also regard it as a strong source of information, provided that users verify what they find.",
