    id: "w-task2-it-benefits-outweigh",
    title: "IT Benefits Outweigh",
    task: "task2",
    type: "advantages-disadvantages",
    topic: "work",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Information technology is changing many aspects of our lives and now dominates our home, leisure and work activities. To what extent do the benefits of information technology outweigh the disadvantages?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Information technology has become central to domestic life, entertainment, and employment. Although this dependence creates risks to privacy, attention, and job security, I believe the benefits outweigh the disadvantages because digital systems greatly expand access to services, knowledge, and flexible work.\n\nThe main drawbacks arise when convenience develops into dependence. At home and during leisure time, constant notifications interrupt conversation and encourage passive screen use, which can displace exercise, sleep, and sustained concentration. Digital platforms collect extensive personal data, leaving users vulnerable to manipulation, fraud, or surveillance. In the workplace, automation can remove routine positions faster than affected employees can retrain, while remote communication may blur the boundary between employment and private life. An office worker who receives messages throughout the evening, for example, gains flexibility during the day but may never feel fully off duty.\n\nNevertheless, information technology produces important gains. Online banking, public-service portals, and telemedicine allow people to complete essential tasks without lengthy travel, which is especially valuable for rural residents and those with disabilities. Digital tools also make education and professional knowledge available at low cost, enabling workers to update their skills as industries change. At work, shared platforms support collaboration across locations and allow employees to organise their hours around caring responsibilities. Even automation, despite disrupting particular roles, can remove repetitive or dangerous tasks and increase productivity, creating resources for new services and occupations. More importantly, most of these benefits affect people's basic ability to access healthcare, education and employment, whereas many of the disadvantages can be reduced through regulation and responsible use.\n\nIn conclusion, the dominance of information technology can undermine privacy, well-being, and employment stability. On balance, however, its contribution to accessible services, learning, safer production, and flexible participation is greater, particularly when sensible protections accompany its use.",
