    id: "w-task2-job-satisfaction-factors",
    title: "Job Satisfaction Factors",
    task: "task2",
    type: "two-part",
    topic: "work",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>As most people spend a major part of their adult life at work, job satisfaction is an important element of individual wellbeing. What factors contribute to job satisfaction? How realistic is the expectation of job satisfaction for all workers?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Because work occupies much of adult life, job satisfaction strongly affects personal wellbeing. It generally arises from meaningful duties, fair conditions, and opportunities to progress, although expecting every worker to feel satisfied at all times is unrealistic.\n\nSeveral connected factors make a job rewarding. Employees are more engaged when they understand how their work benefits customers, colleagues, or the wider community, because daily tasks then carry a clear purpose. Fair pay and reasonable hours also matter: if workers can meet basic needs without constant exhaustion, they have more energy to perform well and maintain life outside work. In addition, supportive managers who recognise good performance and offer useful feedback create trust. This encourages employees to take responsibility and develop new skills, which can lead to promotion and reinforce their sense that effort is worthwhile. A nurse, for example, may accept demanding shifts when staffing is adequate, supervisors are respectful, and professional development is available.\n\nHowever, universal job satisfaction is an improbable goal. Some necessary occupations involve repetitive, dangerous, or emotionally difficult tasks that cannot be made enjoyable simply through better management. Individual expectations also differ: a secure position may satisfy one person but frustrate another who values creativity or rapid advancement. Moreover, employers operate under financial and practical constraints, so they cannot always provide high salaries, flexible schedules, and continuous promotion simultaneously. Nevertheless, widespread dissatisfaction is not inevitable. Even where the work itself is limited, safe conditions, respectful treatment, predictable hours, and a voice in workplace decisions can remove major sources of resentment and make employment tolerable or even fulfilling.\n\nIn conclusion, purpose, fair rewards, humane conditions, and prospects for growth are central to job satisfaction. Complete satisfaction for all workers is unrealistic, but organisations can still ensure that most employees experience dignity, security, and some value in their work.",
