    id: "w-task2-longer-life-expectancy-ad",
    title: "Longer Life Expectancy",
    task: "task2",
    type: "advantages-disadvantages",
    topic: "health",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>One of the consequences of improved medical care is that people are living longer and life expectancy is increasing. Do you think the advantages of this outweigh the disadvantages?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Better medical care has enabled people to survive illnesses that were once fatal, thereby increasing life expectancy. Although this trend places pressure on public services, I believe its social and economic benefits are considerably greater.\n\nOn the one hand, an ageing population can create serious financial demands. As the proportion of retirees rises relative to the working population, governments collect less income tax while spending more on pensions, hospitals and long-term care. This imbalance may require higher taxes or reduced investment in services used by younger citizens, such as schools and public transport. Families can also be affected when elderly relatives need daily assistance, since working adults may have to reduce their hours or pay for professional care. Longer lives are therefore not automatically healthier or financially secure lives.\n\nOn the other hand, greater longevity allows individuals to contribute to their families and society for much longer. Medical advances increasingly prevent disability as well as death, meaning many older adults remain independent, work beyond the traditional retirement age or provide childcare for grandchildren. Their support enables younger parents to stay in employment, which raises household income and preserves valuable skills in the labour market. Longer life expectancy also changes incentives earlier in life: when people expect a lengthy retirement, they have stronger reasons to exercise, save and continue learning. Japan, for example, has created opportunities for older residents to take part in community work and flexible employment rather than treating them solely as dependants. If retirement and healthcare systems adapt in this way, additional years become a social resource instead of merely a public expense.\n\nIn conclusion, rising care costs and pressure on families are genuine disadvantages of longer life expectancy. Nevertheless, the continued economic, practical and social contribution of healthier older people means that the advantages outweigh these concerns.",
