    id: "w-task2-nature-vs-nurture",
    title: "Nature Vs Nurture",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "society",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Research indicates that the characteristics we are born with have much more influence on our personality and development than any experiences we may have in our life. Which do you consider to be the major influence?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Some research suggests that inherited characteristics shape personality and development more strongly than life experience. While biological traits provide an important starting point, I consider upbringing, education and social conditions to be the greater influence because they determine how natural tendencies are expressed and developed.\n\nAdmittedly, individuals are born with different temperaments and abilities. One child may naturally be more sociable, cautious or physically coordinated than another, and these differences can affect the activities they initially prefer. Inherited characteristics may also make certain forms of learning easier, giving some people an early advantage in areas such as music or sport. However, a predisposition does not guarantee a particular outcome. A naturally confident child may become withdrawn in a hostile environment, while a cautious one can learn to communicate effectively through repeated encouragement and practice. Biology therefore sets possibilities rather than fixing a person’s future.\n\nLife experience has a broader influence because it continuously reinforces or redirects these initial tendencies. Parents first shape behaviour by rewarding cooperation, setting boundaries and modelling how to respond to other people. Schools then expand this process by exposing children to new knowledge, expectations and peer groups. Later, employment, relationships and hardship can further alter habits and priorities. A student with modest natural ability, for example, may outperform a more gifted classmate when supportive teaching creates steady motivation and effective study routines. Since experience operates repeatedly across different stages of life, its cumulative effect can transform both competence and character.\n\nIn conclusion, inherited qualities influence people’s early tendencies and potential. Nevertheless, experience is the major force because social environments determine whether those qualities are strengthened, suppressed or redirected throughout life.",
