    id: "w-task2-news-editors-decisions",
    title: "News Editors Decisions",
    task: "task2",
    type: "two-part",
    topic: "media",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>News editors decide what to broadcast on television and what to print in newspapers. What factors do you think influence these decisions? Do we become used to bad news? Would it be better if more good news was reported?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "News organisations receive far more material than they can publish, so editors must make selective decisions. These choices are mainly influenced by audience interest and journalistic importance. Although constant exposure can make people less sensitive to bad news, reporting more positive stories would be beneficial if serious issues were not neglected.\n\nThe commercial need to attract an audience strongly affects news selection. Reports involving conflict, danger or famous individuals create immediate curiosity, encouraging people to click on an article or continue watching a programme. Greater attention then produces advertising income, so dramatic stories receive prominent coverage. Editors must also consider public significance and practical constraints. A policy change affecting millions deserves attention even if it is not entertaining, while an unverified claim cannot responsibly be published. Television additionally favours events for which clear footage is available, meaning that a visually striking incident may displace a more important but less accessible story.\n\nThese pressures explain why negative events appear so frequently and why audiences can gradually become accustomed to them. When reports of crime or disaster are repeated daily, viewers may begin to treat human suffering as ordinary rather than urgent. More good news could counter this effect by showing that social action produces results. Coverage of a successful vaccination campaign, for example, can restore public confidence and encourage participation in similar programmes. However, positive reporting should broaden the news agenda rather than turn journalism into reassurance. If editors avoided corruption or conflict merely to create an uplifting atmosphere, the media would no longer hold powerful institutions accountable.\n\nIn conclusion, editors are guided by commercial appeal, public relevance and the availability of reliable material. A better balance of constructive and negative reporting would reduce desensitisation without weakening journalism’s essential scrutiny.",
