    type: "discussion",
    topic: "work",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Some people work for the same organisation throughout their working life. Others think that it is better to work for different organisations. Discuss both these views and give your own opinion.</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Some people value spending their entire career with one organisation, whereas others prefer to change employers. Although long service can provide stability and deep institutional knowledge, I believe most workers benefit more from moving between organisations at carefully chosen points in their careers.\n\nOn the one hand, remaining with one employer can create both security and professional depth. Employees who understand an organisation’s systems, clients and history can make decisions efficiently because they do not repeatedly spend time adapting to unfamiliar procedures. Years of dependable service may also build trust with senior managers, leading to promotion and greater responsibility. In addition, stable employment makes it easier to plan major commitments such as housing or family expenses. For workers in organisations that offer fair pay, development and varied internal roles, changing employer may therefore involve unnecessary risk.\n\nOn the other hand, experience across several workplaces usually produces broader skills and stronger bargaining power. Each organisation uses different technology, management practices and ways of solving problems. Moving between them exposes employees to these differences, requiring them to adapt and preventing their knowledge from becoming too narrow. They can then transfer effective practices from one workplace to another and offer greater value to future employers. External moves may also be necessary when promotion is blocked or salaries fail to reflect increased ability. A software developer, for example, may progress faster by joining a company using newer systems than by waiting indefinitely for suitable projects at the original firm. Provided that changes are purposeful rather than frequent and impulsive, mobility can improve both learning and long-term career prospects.\n\nIn conclusion, a lifelong career in one organisation offers valuable stability and specialised knowledge. Nevertheless, I favour working for several employers because selective moves generally widen expertise and create better opportunities for advancement.",
