    id: "w-task2-online-socialising",
    title: "Online Socialising",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "society",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Nowadays, more people are choosing to socialize online rather than face to face. Is this a positive or negative development?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "An increasing number of people prefer online interaction to meeting others in person. Although digital communication makes relationships easier to maintain across distance, I believe this preference is largely negative because it can weaken conversational skills and produce less meaningful connections.\n\nAdmittedly, online platforms remove practical barriers to social contact. Friends and relatives in distant places can communicate regularly without the cost of travel. Digital groups also allow people with uncommon interests or health limitations to find supportive communities unavailable locally. For migrants, video calls and group messages can preserve family relationships while helping them build a new life. However, these advantages are greatest when online contact supplements face-to-face interaction. If it becomes the preferred form of socialising even among people who live nearby, convenience begins to replace experiences that technology cannot reproduce.\n\nIn-person communication develops abilities and emotional bonds that are difficult to acquire through a screen. During a conversation, people must interpret tone, facial expressions, pauses, and changes in mood, while adjusting their own responses immediately. Regular practice builds empathy and confidence in handling disagreement. Online exchanges, by contrast, can be edited, delayed, or abandoned, allowing users to avoid discomfort rather than resolve it. They compete with notifications and other content, so frequent contact may receive little genuine attention. A group of friends can exchange messages throughout the day yet know less about one another’s difficulties than they would after one uninterrupted meal together. Over time, choosing digital convenience by default may therefore leave individuals socially connected but emotionally isolated.\n\nIn conclusion, online communication is valuable for overcoming distance and connecting people with particular needs. Nevertheless, preferring it to face-to-face socialising is a negative development because it reduces opportunities to practise essential interpersonal skills and form deeper relationships.",
