    id: "w-task2-online-university-courses",
    title: "Online University Courses",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "education",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Some universities now offer their courses on the Internet so that people can study online. Is this a positive or negative development?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "An increasing number of universities deliver courses through the Internet. I regard this as a largely positive development because it widens access and makes study more flexible, although online provision cannot fully replace campus-based education.\n\nThe clearest advantage is that digital courses remove geographical and scheduling barriers. Students in remote areas can attend lectures from institutions that would otherwise require relocation, while employees and parents can study around existing responsibilities. Recorded material also allows learners to pause difficult explanations and review them at their own pace, which is rarely possible in a lecture hall. These features can make higher education available to people who were previously excluded by distance, disability or inflexible timetables. For example, a working nurse can complete an online public-health module without leaving employment, then apply the knowledge directly in the workplace. Universities may also reach more students without constructing additional classrooms, potentially reducing delivery costs.\n\nNevertheless, the benefits depend on the course and the quality of its design. Students who learn entirely online may have fewer spontaneous discussions with lecturers and classmates, weakening both academic engagement and the social networks that often support later careers. Practical disciplines present an even clearer limitation: trainee chemists, doctors and engineers need supervised laboratories or clinical experience that a video cannot reproduce. In addition, learners without reliable devices or internet connections may face a new form of exclusion. Universities should therefore combine online lectures with live tutorials, accessible technical support and compulsory in-person sessions where practical training is essential. Used in this blended way, technology extends education without pretending that every element of university life can be digitised.\n\nIn conclusion, online university courses are a positive development because they broaden participation and offer valuable flexibility. Their success, however, requires effective teaching and continued face-to-face provision for interaction and practical skills.",
