    id: "w-task2-parents-vs-schools-socialise",
    title: "Parents Vs Schools Socialise",
    task: "task2",
    type: "discussion",
    topic: "education",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Some people think that parents should teach children how to be good members of society. Others, however, believe that school is the place to learn this. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.</p>",
    modelAnswer: "People disagree about whether children should learn responsible social behaviour mainly at home or at school. Although schools play an important role in reinforcing shared standards, I believe parents have the primary responsibility because a child’s earliest and most continuous lessons come from family life.\n\nOn the one hand, schools are well placed to teach children how to function within a wider community. A classroom brings together pupils from different backgrounds and requires them to follow common rules, share limited resources and resolve disagreements. Through group projects, sports and student councils, children experience the consequences of either cooperating with or excluding others. Teachers can also present civic principles systematically, including respect for public property and the rights of people outside one’s immediate circle. Since these lessons occur among peers rather than relatives, pupils can practise conduct that will later be expected in workplaces and public life.\n\nOn the other hand, parents exert a deeper influence because social habits begin long before formal civic education. Children constantly observe how adults speak to service workers, treat neighbours and respond when they make a mistake. If parents model patience and require a child to apologise or help with household duties, repeated practice gradually turns abstract values into normal behaviour. By contrast, a school lesson about honesty is unlikely to be effective if dishonesty is tolerated at home. Parents also know their child’s character closely, allowing them to correct selfish or aggressive behaviour as soon as it appears. Schools should therefore reinforce these foundations and provide a broader social setting, but they cannot replace consistent guidance at home.\n\nIn conclusion, schools offer valuable opportunities to learn cooperation and citizenship among peers. Nevertheless, parents should remain chiefly responsible for raising good members of society, while schools strengthen the values established within the family.",
