    id: "w-task2-petrol-price-traffic-pollution",
    title: "Petrol Price Traffic Pollution",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "environment",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Increasing the price of petrol is the best way to solve growing traffic and pollution problems. To what extent do you agree or disagree?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Raising petrol prices is often proposed as a way to reduce road congestion and pollution. While this policy can discourage some unnecessary journeys, I disagree that it is the best solution because its impact depends on available alternatives and it does not address several major sources of either problem.\n\nAdmittedly, higher fuel costs can change travel behaviour. When each car journey becomes more expensive, commuters have a financial reason to combine errands, share rides or use public transport. Fewer private vehicles would then occupy the roads, which could shorten queues and reduce exhaust emissions. However, this mechanism works only where people have a practical substitute. A nurse travelling before buses begin operating, for instance, may have no choice but to drive and simply bear the additional expense. Petrol increases can therefore place a disproportionate burden on lower-income workers while wealthier motorists continue driving, leaving congestion largely unchanged.\n\nA more effective strategy would make cleaner travel convenient while managing road use directly. Governments can expand reliable bus and rail networks, since frequent and affordable services remove the need to own or use a car. Safe cycling routes and well-planned neighbourhoods can similarly replace short motorised trips. At the same time, congestion charges and limited central parking target vehicles precisely where road space is scarce, rather than penalising every driver regardless of location. Pollution also requires measures beyond private petrol consumption, including stricter emissions standards for factories and commercial vehicles and support for low-emission technology. Together, these policies alter both the choices available to travellers and the environmental performance of the transport system.\n\nIn conclusion, increasing petrol prices may contribute to lower traffic and emissions, but it is neither sufficient nor consistently fair. Better public transport, targeted road management and broader emissions controls offer a more comprehensive solution.",
