    id: "w-task2-prevention-better-than-cure",
    title: "Prevention Better Than Cure",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "health",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>&quot;Prevention is better than cure.&quot; Out of a country&#x27;s health budget, a large proportion should be diverted from treatment to spending on health education and preventative measures. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this statement?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Governments must decide how to divide limited health budgets between treating illness and preventing it. I largely agree that prevention deserves a substantial share of public funding because it reduces both disease and future expenditure, although essential treatment services must remain adequately funded.\n\nIt is true that preventative measures cannot eliminate the need for hospitals and medicine. Accidents, genetic disorders and many serious infections require immediate clinical care regardless of how responsibly people behave. Diverting funds too aggressively could create shortages of staff, beds and equipment, causing treatable conditions to become fatal. Moreover, health education has limited value unless citizens can act on it. Advising low-income families to eat healthier food, for example, achieves little when nutritious products or local medical services remain unaffordable. Any redistribution must therefore preserve an effective treatment system and address practical barriers to healthy choices.\n\nNevertheless, well-designed prevention offers wider and longer-lasting benefits. Education about diet, exercise, vaccination and tobacco use helps individuals recognise risks before illness develops. When this knowledge is supported by screening programmes and accessible vaccines, fewer people reach the stage at which they require expensive specialist care. Resources can then be released for patients whose conditions cannot be prevented. Preventative spending also protects whole communities: high vaccination coverage interrupts transmission, including among people who cannot safely be vaccinated. Thus, investment at an early stage creates a chain of benefits, from healthier behaviour to lower hospital demand and greater financial resilience within the health service.\n\nIn conclusion, a large proportion of health funding should support education and preventative action because these measures reduce avoidable illness and relieve long-term pressure on hospitals. However, this shift should supplement rather than undermine essential treatment.",
