    id: "w-task2-private-school-tax",
    title: "Private School Tax",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "education",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Families who send their children to private schools should not be required to pay taxes that support the state education system. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this statement?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Some argue that parents who purchase private education should be exempt from taxes used to fund state schools. I disagree because taxation supports shared public services, and such an exemption would be both unfair and damaging to society.\n\nAdmittedly, the proposal may appear reasonable from an individual household’s perspective. Private-school parents pay substantial fees and do not use a state-funded place, so requiring them to finance both systems can seem like charging twice for the same service. A tax reduction might also make private education accessible to more middle-income families and reduce pressure on crowded public schools. However, this reasoning treats tax as a direct payment for personal consumption. Citizens contribute to many services they may not use at a particular time, including emergency care, public transport and unemployment support, because these institutions remain available when needed and protect the wider community.\n\nMore importantly, universal contributions are essential to an effective state education system. Public schools educate the majority of children, including those whose families cannot afford alternatives, and require stable funding for qualified teachers, safe buildings and learning materials. If affluent households could withdraw their contribution, the tax base would shrink while resources became concentrated among children who were already advantaged. This would widen educational inequality and eventually affect everyone through lower social mobility and a less skilled workforce. Moreover, private-school families benefit indirectly from publicly educated nurses, engineers and other professionals. Supporting their education is therefore not charity towards unrelated households but an investment in the society on which all citizens depend.\n\nIn conclusion, although private-school fees impose a genuine cost on parents, this does not justify excluding them from education taxes. State schooling is a collective institution whose social and economic benefits extend far beyond the families using it directly.",
