    id: "w-task2-punishment-right-wrong",
    title: "Punishment Right Wrong",
    task: "task2",
    type: "two-part",
    topic: "education",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>It is important for children to learn the difference between right and wrong at an early age. Punishment is necessary to help them learn this distinction. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this opinion? What sort of punishment should parents and teachers be allowed to use to teach good behaviour to children?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Children need to understand right and wrong from an early age, and reasonable punishment can contribute to this process. I therefore partly agree that it is necessary, but only when it takes the form of proportionate, non-violent consequences that explain and correct misconduct.\n\nPunishment has value because moral rules can otherwise seem optional to a young child. If deliberate lying or bullying produces no consequence, the immediate benefit of avoiding blame or gaining power may encourage the behaviour to continue. A predictable response shows that harmful choices carry costs and protects other children at the same time. Nevertheless, punishment alone cannot teach morality. Fear may stop a child while an adult is present, but it does not explain why the action was wrong. Parents and teachers must therefore connect any consequence to a calm explanation of the harm caused and show the child what an acceptable response would have been.\n\nThe permitted measures should be limited, relevant and designed to repair behaviour. Parents might temporarily withdraw privileges, such as recreational screen time, while teachers could assign a short detention or remove a pupil from an activity. Where possible, the consequence should also match the offence: a child who deliberately creates a mess should help clean it, while one who damages another pupil’s work should apologise and assist in replacing it. This link encourages responsibility because the child must confront and remedy the actual harm. Physical punishment, humiliation and prolonged isolation should never be allowed, as they model aggression, damage trust and may teach children merely to conceal mistakes.\n\nIn conclusion, carefully chosen punishment can help children distinguish right from wrong when it is combined with explanation and guidance. Adults should use mild loss of privileges and restorative consequences, but violence or degrading treatment is both unnecessary and harmful.",
