    id: "w-task2-scanners-cctv-privacy-ad",
    title: "Scanners CCTV Privacy",
    task: "task2",
    type: "advantages-disadvantages",
    topic: "technology",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Some people believe that technological tools such as body scanners and CCTV have significantly enhanced our safety and security, while others feel that they have resulted in a loss of privacy. Do the benefits of these items outweigh their disadvantages?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Body scanners, security cameras and similar technologies are now widely used to detect threats and record public activity. Although these tools can intrude on privacy, I believe their safety benefits are greater when data collection is limited, transparent and subject to independent oversight.\n\nThe principal disadvantage is that security systems may collect sensitive information from people who have done nothing wrong. Body scanners can reveal personal physical details, while connected CCTV networks can create a record of an individual's movements. If these images are retained indefinitely or shared without permission, they may be used for purposes unrelated to public safety. Awareness of constant observation can also discourage behaviour such as attending a peaceful demonstration. These risks are serious because citizens have little control over information once it enters a government or corporate database. Clear retention periods and restrictions on secondary use are therefore essential.\n\nNevertheless, carefully deployed technology provides protection. Airport scanners can identify concealed weapons that a search may miss, reducing the likelihood of a serious incident without requiring every passenger to undergo an invasive physical examination. CCTV can deter opportunistic crime and, when an offence occurs, give investigators reliable evidence about the time, location and people involved. Cameras in railway stations may also help staff identify a passenger who has fallen onto the tracks or locate a missing child quickly. These benefits are immediate and can prevent irreversible harm, whereas many privacy risks can be reduced through encryption, restricted access and penalties for misuse. The appropriate response is therefore strict regulation rather than the removal of effective security tools.\n\nIn conclusion, scanners and CCTV can expose personal information and enable excessive surveillance. However, their capacity to prevent threats and support rapid intervention outweighs these disadvantages, provided that authorities collect only necessary data and remain accountable for its use.",
