    id: "w-task2-shopping-as-hobby",
    title: "Shopping As Hobby",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "society",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>In the past, shopping was a routine domestic task. Many people nowadays regard it as a hobby. To what extent do you think this is a positive trend?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Shopping was once a practical activity undertaken to obtain household necessities, whereas many people now treat it as a leisure pursuit. Although this shift can support social interaction, I consider it largely negative because it encourages unnecessary consumption.\n\nAdmittedly, recreational shopping can offer benefits when it remains controlled. Visiting a market or shopping centre allows friends and relatives to spend time together, particularly where other leisure facilities are scarce. Consumers may also enjoy comparing products, discovering local businesses, or choosing gifts, turning an ordinary errand into a pleasant experience. This activity also supports retailers: greater customer traffic helps shops retain staff and keeps commercial districts active. Weekend pedestrian streets in many cities, for example, combine small stores with food and entertainment, creating lively spaces that serve a broader social purpose.\n\nHowever, treating consumption itself as entertainment changes the reason people buy. Instead of identifying a need and then selecting a suitable product, habitual shoppers browse for stimulation and are repeatedly exposed to discounts, advertising, and rapidly changing fashions. These prompts create desire; desire leads to impulsive purchases; and repeated spending can eventually place pressure on household budgets. The consequences extend beyond personal finance. When goods are bought for brief satisfaction rather than lasting use, they are more likely to be discarded while still functional. Manufacturers then produce replacement items to meet artificial demand, consuming additional energy and raw materials and generating more packaging and waste. The problem is therefore not that shopping can be enjoyable, but that enjoyment becomes tied to acquiring ever more possessions.\n\nIn conclusion, shopping as a hobby can provide social contact and strengthen local retail areas. Nevertheless, its tendency to promote impulsive spending and disposable consumption makes this development negative overall, especially when purchasing becomes a regular source of entertainment.",
