    type: "two-part",
    topic: "technology",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Many people nowadays spend a large part of their free time using a smartphone. What do you think are the reasons for this? Do you think this is a positive or negative development?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Smartphones now occupy much of many people's leisure time. This pattern is driven by the convenience and persuasive design of mobile applications, and I believe it is negative overall because it displaces restorative and socially meaningful activities.\n\nThe first reason for prolonged use is that one device provides almost every form of entertainment. Rather than arranging an outing or turning on a computer, users can watch videos, play games, read news, and contact friends within seconds. This convenience removes practical barriers that once limited media consumption. More importantly, many platforms are designed to retain attention. Personalised recommendations supply an endless stream of relevant content, while notifications repeatedly encourage users to return. A commuter who opens an app to answer one message, for example, may remain online for half an hour because each short video leads automatically to another. Smartphone use therefore expands to fill unplanned leisure.\n\nAlthough this technology offers affordable relaxation and helps distant friends remain connected, its effect is harmful when it dominates free time. Constant low-effort stimulation makes slower activities, such as reading, exercising, or learning an instrument, seem less rewarding. People then practise them less often, losing opportunities to develop concentration, physical fitness, and practical skills. Heavy phone use can also weaken face-to-face relationships. If family members repeatedly check separate screens during meals, conversation becomes fragmented, and their shared time no longer provides the same emotional connection. These costs arise from the displacement that occurs when a smartphone becomes the default response to every quiet moment.\n\nIn conclusion, smartphones consume so much leisure time because they combine exceptional convenience with applications engineered to sustain attention. While moderate use is useful, the present tendency is largely negative because it crowds out activities that support health, sustained focus, and stronger personal relationships.",
