    id: "w-task2-space-exploration-pride-future",
    title: "Space Exploration Pride Future",
    task: "task2",
    type: "two-part",
    topic: "society",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Some people argue that space exploration has had more to do with national pride than international effort. To what extent do you agree with this? How do you think space exploration will change in the future?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Space programmes are often portrayed as competitions through which countries demonstrate their power. I partly agree that national pride has been a major influence, but international cooperation has become equally important, and future missions are likely to involve broader partnerships with private companies.\n\nNational prestige clearly shaped the early space age. During the Cold War, the United States and the Soviet Union treated each successful launch as evidence that their political system and scientific institutions were superior. Landing astronauts on the Moon therefore offered symbolic value far beyond the knowledge gained from the mission. This competitive motive remains visible today, as emerging powers use lunar missions and satellite launches to display technical independence. Nevertheless, modern exploration cannot be explained mainly by pride. Projects such as the International Space Station depend on scientists, funding and equipment from several countries because their cost and complexity exceed what most governments can manage alone.\n\nIn the future, this collaborative model will probably expand, although the participants will change. Governments are increasingly purchasing launch services from commercial firms instead of developing every system themselves. Reusable rockets can lower costs, allowing smaller countries, universities and businesses to place instruments in orbit and contribute to research. At the same time, ambitious missions to the Moon or Mars will require common technical standards and agreements on the use of resources, since unregulated competition could produce conflict or dangerous amounts of space debris. National agencies may still lead prestigious missions, but they will operate within networks that include private manufacturers and international research teams.\n\nIn conclusion, national pride was central to early exploration and continues to influence government investment, but it is no longer the dominant force in every project. Space activity will become more commercial and accessible while requiring stronger international rules and cooperation.",
