    task: "task2",
    type: "discussion",
    topic: "health",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Some people say that the best way to improve public health is by increasing the number of sports facilities. Others, however, say that this would have little effect on public health and that other measures are required. Discuss both these views and give your own opinion.</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Some people argue that more sports facilities are the best way to improve public health, while others believe these would achieve little without broader action. Although accessible facilities can help people exercise more often, I believe measures addressing cost, habits, and the wider environment would produce greater benefits.\n\nOn the one hand, additional sports centres, swimming pools, and public courts can remove a practical barrier to exercise. Nearby facilities reduce travel time and let residents fit physical activity around work or family responsibilities. Regular exercise then becomes easier to maintain, reducing the risk of obesity and related illness. Public spaces can also make sport a social activity, which encourages participants to return because they feel accountable to a group. A neighbourhood court, for example, may support inexpensive evening games for residents who would not join a private gym.\n\nOn the other hand, infrastructure alone does not ensure that it will be used by those with the greatest health risks. People may remain inactive because fees are unaffordable, working hours are long, or they lack confidence and knowledge, so another building may simply serve residents who already exercise. Public health also depends heavily on diet, tobacco use, pollution, and preventive care. Governments should therefore combine affordable exercise programmes with clear nutrition education, restrictions on harmful products, safer walking routes, and accessible health checks. These measures change both individual choices and the conditions surrounding them. If healthy food is affordable and streets are safe, for instance, better habits become realistic parts of daily life rather than occasional activities requiring special facilities.\n\nIn conclusion, more sports facilities can support exercise and community participation, but their effect is limited when financial and behavioural barriers remain. A broader policy that makes healthy choices affordable, understandable, and convenient is therefore more likely to improve public health.",
