    task: "task2",
    type: "discussion",
    topic: "sport",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Successful sports professionals can earn a great deal more money than people in other important professions. Some people think this is fully justified while others think it is unfair. Discuss both these views and give your own opinion.</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Successful sports professionals often earn far more than people in essential jobs such as teaching, nursing, or scientific research. Some people see this as unfair, while others believe it is justified. Both views have force, and I believe the high pay is largely justified by market demand, even if it sits uncomfortably beside more socially vital work.\n\nOn the one hand, it is understandable that many people find these salaries unfair. Surgeons, teachers, and engineers take on responsibilities that protect health, educate the next generation, or keep infrastructure running, yet their pay rarely matches that of top athletes. When the media constantly shows sports stars’ wealth, the gap can look like a sign that society rewards entertainment more than public service. From this angle, the issue is not envy of success, but a sense that financial reward is poorly aligned with social value.\n\nOn the other hand, sports pay is driven less by moral ranking than by what audiences and sponsors are willing to pay. Only a small number of athletes reach the top, and their careers are often short and physically demanding, with intense public pressure and little privacy. Clubs and broadcasters earn large revenues from fans, so salaries rise with popularity rather than with a government scale of “importance”. In this sense, huge earnings reflect scarce talent and commercial demand. That does not mean other professions are less valuable; it means sports markets price fame and performance differently from public-sector work.\n\nIn conclusion, while comparing athletes with essential professionals makes the pay gap feel unfair, I believe top sports salaries are largely justified by short careers, intense competition, and the money sport itself generates.",
