    id: "w-task2-student-behaviour-causes-solutions",
    title: "Student Behaviour Causes Solutions",
    task: "task2",
    type: "problem-solution",
    topic: "education",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>In many countries schools have severe problems with student behavior. What do you think are the causes of this? What solutions can you suggest?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Serious behavioural problems among schoolchildren have become a concern in many education systems. They are largely caused by inconsistent discipline at home and disengagement at school, so effective responses must involve both families and educational institutions.\n\nOne major cause is the absence of clear boundaries outside the classroom. When parents apply rules inconsistently or rarely challenge disrespectful conduct, children may enter school without understanding that actions have consequences. Teachers then have to establish standards that are not reinforced at home, making repeated disruption more likely. A second factor is that some pupils feel disconnected from their lessons. Classes that rely heavily on passive memorisation can appear irrelevant, particularly to students who struggle academically. Frustration then develops into boredom, and disruptive behaviour becomes a way to gain attention or avoid difficult tasks. Online content can intensify both problems by normalising aggressive language and shortening pupils’ tolerance for sustained concentration.\n\nSolutions should address these causes directly. Schools and parents should first agree on a small set of behavioural expectations and predictable consequences. Regular communication would allow minor incidents to be dealt with consistently before they develop into persistent misconduct. Schools should also make lessons more engaging through practical tasks, structured discussion and support for pupils who are falling behind. When students can participate successfully, they are less likely to seek attention through disruption. In addition, counselling and supervised digital-literacy sessions could help pupils manage anger and recognise harmful online influences. A student involved in repeated conflicts, for example, may benefit more from targeted counselling combined with firm consequences than from suspension alone.\n\nIn conclusion, poor behaviour often results from weak boundaries and academic disengagement. Consistent cooperation with families, more accessible teaching and appropriate pastoral support can therefore improve conduct more effectively than punishment in isolation.",
