    id: "w-task2-surveillance-technology-ad",
    title: "Surveillance Technology",
    task: "task2",
    type: "advantages-disadvantages",
    topic: "technology",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Nowadays technology is increasingly being used to monitor what people are saying and doing (for example, through cellphone tracking and security cameras). In many cases, the people being monitored are unaware that this is happening. Do you think the advantages of this development outweigh the disadvantages?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Digital systems now allow organisations to track people's movements and communications, often without their knowledge. Although such monitoring can improve security, I believe its disadvantages outweigh the benefits when meaningful consent and strict oversight are absent.\n\nThe strongest argument for surveillance is that it helps authorities prevent harm and respond when incidents occur. Security cameras can deter theft, provide evidence after an offence and help police locate a missing person. Similarly, anonymised cellphone location data can reveal where crowds are forming, allowing transport agencies to adjust services or emergency teams to plan evacuations. During a natural disaster, for example, information about population movement may direct rescue workers towards areas where people remain trapped. In these cases, monitoring shortens the gap between a problem arising and assistance reaching those affected, so carefully limited use can protect the public.\n\nHowever, invisible monitoring creates more fundamental risks. People cannot challenge the collection or misuse of information when they do not know it is occurring, and data gathered for one purpose can later be sold, leaked or used to judge them in another context. Location histories may expose medical visits, religious activity or private relationships even when a person has broken no law. This knowledge also changes behaviour: if citizens believe every conversation or movement may be recorded, they may avoid lawful protests or sensitive discussions. As a result, a tool intended to provide safety can gradually weaken freedom and transfer excessive power to governments and technology companies. Unlike a single security failure, a permanent surveillance system is difficult to reverse once its infrastructure and databases are established.\n\nIn conclusion, monitoring technology has legitimate value in emergencies and criminal investigations. Nevertheless, covert and weakly regulated surveillance threatens privacy, free expression and the balance of power so seriously that its disadvantages are greater.",
