    id: "w-task2-talent-vs-training",
    title: "Talent Vs Training",
    task: "task2",
    type: "discussion",
    topic: "education",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>It is generally believed that some people are born with certain talents, for instance for sport or music, and others are not. However, it is sometimes claimed that any child can be taught to become a good sports person or musician. Discuss both these views and give your own opinion.</p>",
    modelAnswer: "It is widely believed that ability in fields such as sport and music is partly inherited, while others argue that any child can be trained to perform well. I believe natural aptitude provides an advantage, but sustained, high-quality training is the decisive factor for most young people.\n\nOn the one hand, inborn characteristics can influence how quickly someone develops. A child may possess an unusually accurate sense of pitch, excellent coordination, or a body type suited to a particular sport. These qualities make early practice more rewarding because progress comes faster, which can strengthen motivation and attract further support from teachers or coaches. At the highest professional level, small physical or perceptual advantages may separate exceptional performers from merely competent ones. It is therefore unrealistic to assume that every child begins with identical potential or can reach the same elite standard.\n\nOn the other hand, talent produces little without structured practice. Technical skills such as controlling a football, reading music, or maintaining rhythm are learned through repeated correction rather than inherited fully formed. Effective coaching identifies weaknesses, divides complex actions into manageable stages, and gives children feedback that gradually turns mistakes into reliable habits. Training also develops concentration and resilience, enabling learners to continue when improvement becomes slower. A naturally gifted pianist who rarely practises may therefore be overtaken by a less obviously talented pupil who works consistently with a good teacher. Although not every child can become an international star, most can reach a strong level when instruction is appropriate and opportunities are sustained.\n\nIn conclusion, natural talent affects the speed and possible ceiling of achievement, especially in elite competition. However, because training converts potential into dependable performance, it remains the more decisive influence on whether most children become good musicians or sportspeople.",
