    id: "w-task2-tv-sport-participation",
    title: "TV Sport Participation",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "sport",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>In many parts of the world, there is continuous coverage of sport on television. Some people believe this discourages the young from taking part in any sport themselves. Discuss this view and give your own opinion.</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Television channels devote considerable airtime to sport, leading to concerns that young viewers may watch rather than participate. Although excessive viewing can encourage a sedentary routine, I believe sports coverage generally motivates participation, provided that opportunities are available.\n\nThe concern is understandable because televised sport can become a substitute for physical activity. Major competitions and related analysis are broadcast for hours. As this passive entertainment occupies more of their leisure time, fewer hours remain for training or informal games with friends. The effect may be stronger when professional sport is presented mainly as a spectacle involving exceptionally talented athletes. Some viewers may conclude that meaningful participation requires elite ability and therefore avoid joining a local team where they seem modest by comparison. Continuous coverage can thus reinforce inactivity among children who already lack confidence or encouragement.\n\nNevertheless, watching sport often provides the initial interest that leads young people to participate. Broadcasts make unfamiliar activities visible, explain their rules, and present athletes whose discipline can be admired. This exposure creates curiosity; curiosity prompts children to try the activity; and an enjoyable first experience can develop into a lasting habit. Football participation, for example, commonly rises around major international tournaments because children want to imitate the skills they have seen. More importantly, television cannot determine behaviour on its own. If schools offer affordable clubs and neighbourhoods provide safe facilities, enthusiasm generated on screen has somewhere practical to go. By contrast, even highly motivated viewers will remain spectators when equipment, coaching, or suitable spaces are inaccessible.\n\nIn conclusion, constant sports broadcasting can displace exercise and intimidate some inexperienced viewers. However, its overall influence is more likely to be positive because it generates interest and supplies role models; low participation is better explained by limited opportunities than by television itself.",
