    id: "w-task2-university-subjects-choice",
    title: "University Subjects Choice",
    task: "task2",
    type: "discussion",
    topic: "education",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Some people think that all university students should study whatever they like. Others believe that they should only be allowed to study subjects that will be useful in the future, such as those related to science and technology. Discuss both these views and give your own opinion.</p>",
    modelAnswer: "People disagree over whether university students should freely select their subjects or be restricted to fields with future value, especially science and technology. Although governments may encourage study in shortage areas, I believe students should retain the final choice.\n\nOn the one hand, limiting places to economically useful disciplines could help countries address urgent skill shortages. Training more engineers, data specialists and healthcare professionals may strengthen infrastructure, innovation and public services. This is especially persuasive where university education is heavily subsidised, since taxpayers can expect public investment to support national needs rather than produce graduates for already crowded occupations. Furthermore, students do not always have accurate information about employment prospects, so directing them towards expanding sectors might protect some from costly choices followed by unemployment. From this perspective, restrictions appear to align educational supply with future labour demand.\n\nOn the other hand, compulsory subject selection is likely to be ineffective and unnecessarily narrow. University courses require sustained effort, and students forced into an unsuitable field may lose motivation, perform poorly or leave before graduating. Freedom of choice allows individuals to match study with their abilities, increasing the likelihood that they will develop expertise. More importantly, future usefulness cannot be reduced to science and technology. Societies also require teachers, lawyers, translators and artists, while skills developed in history or philosophy, such as evaluating evidence and constructing arguments, remain valuable across occupations. Labour markets also change too quickly for governments to predict every long-term need. A better approach is to publish reliable career information and offer scholarships for shortage subjects while preserving voluntary choice.\n\nIn conclusion, prioritising science and technology may appear to deliver economic benefits, particularly when public funds are involved. Nevertheless, informed student choice is more likely to produce committed graduates and a balanced range of skills than rigid restrictions.",
