    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "health",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Some people choose to eat no meat or fish. They believe that this is not only better for their own health but also benefits the world as a whole. Discuss this view and give your own opinion.</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Some people avoid meat and fish because they regard a vegetarian diet as healthier and less damaging to the planet. Although this choice can produce both benefits, I believe they depend on replacing animal products with nutritious foods and on how those foods are produced.\n\nFrom a health perspective, a well-planned vegetarian diet can reduce reliance on processed meat, which is often high in salt and saturated fat. Replacing it with beans, whole grains, vegetables, and nuts increases fibre intake and can help people maintain a healthy weight. This lowers the likelihood of conditions associated with poor diets, including heart disease. However, simply removing meat does not guarantee good health. Someone who mainly consumes refined carbohydrates, fried snacks, and sugary drinks remains exposed to serious nutritional problems. Moreover, without careful meal planning, vegetarians may lack iron, vitamin B12, or sufficient protein. The health argument is therefore persuasive only when exclusion is accompanied by balanced substitution.\n\nThe wider environmental case is stronger. Raising livestock requires extensive land, feed, and water, while cattle farming also produces substantial greenhouse-gas emissions. When demand for meat falls, less forest needs to be cleared for grazing or animal feed, which protects habitats and reduces pressure on natural resources. Plant-based school meals, for example, can lower emissions across thousands of servings without requiring every student to become vegetarian. Admittedly, imported fruit grown with heavy irrigation may also carry an environmental cost. Nevertheless, staple plant foods generally require fewer resources than livestock, so broad dietary change would still benefit the world overall.\n\nIn conclusion, vegetarianism can improve personal health if meals remain nutritionally complete, and its reduced demand for resource-intensive livestock offers clear global gains. I therefore support this dietary choice, while recognising that responsible food selection matters as much as avoiding meat itself.",
