    id: "w-task2-women-work-juvenile-delinquency",
    title: "Women Work Juvenile Delinquency",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "society",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>The position of women in society has changed markedly in the last twenty years. Many of the problems young people now experience, such as juvenile delinquency, arise from the fact that many married women now work and are not at home to care for their children. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this opinion?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "The growing participation of married women in employment is sometimes blamed for behavioural problems among young people. While insufficient parental attention can place children at risk, I disagree that working mothers are a major cause of juvenile delinquency. This claim overlooks shared parental responsibility and stronger social influences.\n\nChildren clearly need consistent care and supervision. If both parents work excessive hours and make no suitable childcare arrangements, warning signs such as poor school attendance or association with harmful peer groups may go unnoticed. Minor misconduct can then continue without guidance and develop into more serious behaviour. However, this problem results from a shortage of responsible adult support, not specifically from the mother’s employment. Fathers, grandparents, schools and childcare providers can all offer stability. Indeed, a working mother may improve a child’s security by increasing household income and preventing the financial stress that often damages family relationships.\n\nJuvenile delinquency usually arises from a combination of wider pressures. Poverty and unequal access to education can leave teenagers with few legitimate routes to status or financial independence. This makes gang membership or petty crime appear more rewarding, especially when such behaviour is reinforced by peers. Young people may also encounter violent online content and local drug markets beyond parental observation. Tackling these influences requires effective schools, safe public spaces and early support for vulnerable families. Expecting mothers to withdraw from employment would neither remove these risks nor guarantee attentive parenting; instead, it could deepen financial hardship and reproduce unequal gender roles.\n\nIn conclusion, a lack of parental involvement can contribute to youth problems, but employment among married women is not their principal cause. Delinquency is better addressed through shared caregiving and stronger social support.",
