    id: "w-task2-young-offenders-adult-punishment",
    title: "Young Offenders Adult Punishment",
    task: "task2",
    type: "opinion",
    topic: "crime",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Some people believe that young people who commit serious crimes should be punished in the same way as adults. To what extent do you agree or disagree?</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Some people argue that young people who commit serious crimes should face the same penalties as adults. I disagree with this view, because their maturity and capacity for reform call for a different, though still firm, response.\n\nAdmittedly, there are good reasons for treating serious youth crime like adult crime. The harm caused by violent assault or robbery does not become smaller simply because the offender is under eighteen, so victims and the wider public may reasonably expect the punishment to reflect how serious the act was, not the offender’s age. From this perspective, a much lighter approach for juveniles can look unfair: two similar offences appear to carry different consequences for reasons unrelated to the damage done. People who support equal punishment therefore see adult-style sentencing as a way to treat similar cases more consistently and to show that serious crimes are judged by their impact, not by youth.\n\nThat said, giving young offenders exactly the same sentences as adults overlooks how adolescents typically make decisions. They are still developing judgment and impulse control, and peer pressure, incomplete schooling, and limited life experience often shape their choices differently from adult crime. Placing them in adult prisons can put them among more experienced offenders, which raises the risk of reoffending rather than reducing it. By contrast, measures suited to their age — such as supervised detention, structured education, and counselling — can confront the offence while still leaving them a realistic path back into society. Many justice systems already separate juvenile courts for this reason: the aim is not to ignore seriousness, but to punish in a way that matches both the crime and the offender’s stage of development.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree that young people who commit serious crimes should be punished in exactly the same way as adults. Firm accountability is necessary, but identical adult penalties overlook their developmental stage and can undermine long-term rehabilitation.",
