    id: "w-task2-youth-financial-literacy",
    title: "Youth Financial Literacy",
    task: "task2",
    type: "problem-solution",
    topic: "education",
    band: "8.0",
    promptHtml: "<p>Nowadays young people lack an understanding of how to manage their finances after they finish high school. Explain why they do not know how to manage money and how this can be changed.</p>",
    modelAnswer: "Many school leavers enter adulthood without the knowledge required to manage money responsibly. This problem largely results from limited financial education and a lack of practical experience, and it can be addressed through relevant school lessons and greater parental involvement.\n\nOne reason for this weakness is that personal finance is rarely treated as a core school subject. Students may learn advanced mathematics, yet receive little guidance on preparing a budget, comparing loan costs, or understanding taxation. Consequently, they can solve abstract equations but remain unprepared for everyday financial decisions. In addition, many teenagers have few opportunities to handle money independently. Parents often pay for essential expenses and may avoid discussing household finances, either to protect their children from worry or because money is considered a private matter. When these young people begin earning or borrowing after graduation, attractive credit offers and online shopping make overspending easy before sound habits have been established.\n\nThe solutions should correspond directly to these causes. Schools could include compulsory financial-literacy modules in which students create monthly budgets, calculate interest, and examine realistic employment contracts. Practical simulations would make the subject more useful than memorising definitions. Families should also give teenagers controlled responsibility before they leave school. For example, a regular allowance could cover transport, meals, and leisure, requiring them to prioritise needs and save for larger purchases. Parents could then review mistakes without immediately replacing all the money spent, so that young people learn from experience while still having guidance at home.\n\nIn conclusion, young adults often struggle because neither schools nor families provide sufficient instruction and practice. Combining practical classroom education with supervised financial responsibility at home would prepare them to make more informed decisions after graduation.",
